/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/az-104.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("az-104", [["adm1",0,1,"A new employee in Marketing must automatically join the Marketing-All group when HR sets their department attribute. What should the administrator configure?",["An assigned security group with an owner who adds each new user","A Microsoft 365 group with guest access enabled","A dynamic user group with a rule on the department attribute","An administrative unit scoped to the Marketing department"],2,"Dynamic user groups add and remove members automatically based on attribute rules. An assigned group needs someone to add members, and an administrative unit only scopes admin permissions.","Manage Entra users and groups",["An assigned group only changes when someone adds or removes members by hand, so new Marketing hires would not join automatically.","Guest access controls whether external users can join a Microsoft 365 group; it does nothing to add members based on the department attribute.",null,"Administrative units scope who can administer a set of users; they do not add users to groups."],2],["adm2",0,1,"A partner's consultant needs to sign in with their own company account to access one resource group in your tenant. What should you do?",["Create a new cloud-only user account in your tenant for the consultant","Invite the consultant as a B2B guest user and assign an RBAC role","Share the resource group's access keys with the consultant","Add the partner's domain as a custom domain in your tenant"],1,"B2B collaboration lets external users sign in with their home identity; you then grant access with RBAC. A separate account duplicates identities, and resource groups have no access keys.","Manage external users",["A separate cloud-only account makes the consultant manage a second password and leaves an identity you must deprovision yourself, instead of letting them use their own company account.",null,"Resource groups have no access keys; keys belong to specific services such as storage accounts, and sharing them bypasses identity-based access.","Adding a custom domain lets your own tenant use that domain name for its users; it does not let accounts from another organization sign in."],2],["adm3",0,1,"A team lead must create and manage all resources in a resource group but must not be able to grant anyone else access. Which built-in role fits?",["Owner","User Access Administrator","Reader","Contributor"],3,"Contributor can manage all resources but cannot assign roles. Owner can also assign roles, and User Access Administrator manages access without managing resources.","Manage access to Azure resources",["Owner includes Microsoft.Authorization write rights, so the team lead could assign roles to others, which the requirement forbids.","User Access Administrator manages role assignments but cannot create or manage resources, which is the opposite of what is needed.","Reader can only view resources, so the team lead could not create or change anything.",null],1],["adm4",0,1,"A help desk engineer must manage role assignments on a subscription but should not create or modify resources. Which role is the best fit?",["User Access Administrator","Owner","Contributor","Security Reader"],0,"User Access Administrator manages access to resources without resource management rights. Owner would also allow resource changes, which breaks least privilege.","Manage access to Azure resources",[null,"Owner can manage role assignments but also create, change and delete resources, which breaks least privilege here.","Contributor can manage resources but cannot manage role assignments, so it fails both parts of the requirement.","Security Reader is a read-only role for security data in Defender for Cloud; it cannot manage role assignments."],2],["adm5",0,1,"A user is assigned Reader at the subscription and Contributor on resource group RG1. What can they do in RG1?",["Only view resources, because the subscription-level role takes priority","Nothing, because conflicting assignments cancel out","Create and modify resources, because RBAC permissions are additive","View resources only until an Owner approves the Contributor role"],2,"Azure RBAC is additive: effective permissions are the union of all assignments at the scope and above, so Contributor applies in RG1. Assignments do not cancel each other.","Interpret access assignments",["Azure RBAC has no priority between scopes; a broader Reader assignment does not cap a narrower Contributor assignment.","Role assignments never cancel out; only a deny assignment can remove permissions that a role grants.",null,"Built-in role assignments take effect immediately; approval workflows exist only if you add something like PIM, which the question does not mention."],2],["adm6",0,1,"An auditor needs to read blob contents in one storage account but must not manage the account. Which role should you assign on the account?",["Reader","Storage Blob Data Reader","Storage Account Contributor","Contributor"],1,"Storage Blob Data Reader grants data-plane read access to blobs. Reader only covers the management plane and cannot read data with Entra authentication, and the contributor roles are far too broad.","Manage access to Azure resources",["Reader covers the management plane only, so it shows the account's settings but does not grant data actions to read blob contents with Entra ID.",null,"Storage Account Contributor manages the account, including its keys, which is far more than read access and gives management rights the auditor must not have.","Contributor can manage every resource in its scope, including listing keys, which is much broader than reading blobs."],2],["adm7",0,1,"Company policy says resources may only be created in East US and West Europe, even by subscription Owners. What should you implement?",["A custom RBAC role without write permissions in other regions","A ReadOnly lock on the subscription","A budget with an alert on other regions","An Azure Policy assignment using Allowed locations"],3,"Azure Policy with the Allowed locations definition denies deployments to other regions regardless of the deployer's role. RBAC controls actions, not locations, and a lock blocks every change.","Implement and manage Azure Policy",["RBAC permissions describe actions, not regions, so a role cannot allow a write in East US but deny the same write elsewhere; Owners would also still hold their own role.","A ReadOnly lock blocks all changes to the subscription's resources, including deployments to the allowed regions.","A budget only tracks cost and sends alerts; it never blocks a deployment in any region.",null],2],["adm8",0,1,"You assigned a policy with the Modify effect that adds a CostCenter tag. New resources get the tag, but existing ones do not. What should you do?",["Create a remediation task for the policy assignment","Reassign the policy with the Audit effect","Apply a CanNotDelete lock on the resource group","Add the tag to the subscription so it inherits down"],0,"Modify and DeployIfNotExists only act on new or updated resources until you run a remediation task, which uses the assignment's managed identity. Tags on a subscription are not inherited.","Implement and manage Azure Policy",[null,"Audit only reports non-compliance; switching to it would stop adding the tag rather than fix existing resources.","A CanNotDelete lock only prevents deletion and has nothing to do with adding tags.","Tags are not inherited from a subscription or resource group, so tagging the subscription does not change the resources."],2],["adm9",0,1,"Which statement about Azure resource tags is correct?",["Tags on a resource group are automatically copied to all of its resources","Tags can only be applied to resources, never to resource groups","Resources do not inherit resource group tags unless a policy applies them","Tags set on a subscription are enforced on all resources under it"],2,"Tags are not inherited. To copy a resource group tag to resources, use a policy such as Inherit a tag from the resource group. Resource groups and subscriptions can have their own tags.","Manage tags",["Resources do not inherit resource group tags automatically; you need a policy such as Inherit a tag from the resource group.","Resource groups and subscriptions can have their own tags, so this restriction is not true.",null,"Subscription tags are not inherited or enforced on child resources; enforcement needs Azure Policy."],1],["adm10",0,1,"A production resource group must never be deleted by accident, but admins must still update resources in it. Which lock should be applied?",["ReadOnly","CanNotDelete","Deny assignment","A Deny policy for deletes"],1,"CanNotDelete blocks deletion but allows reading and modifying. ReadOnly would block updates as well, and a Deny policy evaluates resource properties rather than delete operations (Azure Policy's newer DenyAction effect can block deletes, but a CanNotDelete lock is the direct, simplest control).","Configure resource locks",["ReadOnly blocks every write operation, so admins could no longer update resources in the group.",null,"Deny assignments are created by Azure features such as managed applications and deployment stacks; you cannot create one directly as a simple accidental-delete guard.","The Deny effect evaluates create and update requests, not deletes; only the newer DenyAction effect can block deletes, and a lock is the simpler built-in answer."],1],["adm11",0,1,"After an admin applied a ReadOnly lock to a storage account, an app that lists the account keys began to fail. Why?",["ReadOnly locks also revoke existing SAS tokens","ReadOnly locks force the account to Archive tier","Listing keys requires the Owner role, which the lock removes","Listing keys is a POST operation, which ReadOnly blocks"],3,"ReadOnly blocks any write-type operation, and listing keys uses a POST request, so it fails. The lock does not change roles, tiers or SAS tokens.","Configure resource locks",["Locks do not touch SAS tokens; existing SAS tokens keep working until they expire or their signing key is rotated.","Locks never change a storage account's access tier, and Archive cannot be set as an account default tier anyway.","Locks do not add or remove role assignments; listing keys requires the listKeys action, not specifically Owner.",null],3],["adm12",0,1,"An organization with 40 subscriptions wants to apply the same policies and RBAC assignments to groups of subscriptions by department. What should it use?",["Management groups","Resource groups","Administrative units","Azure Blueprints artifacts on each resource"],0,"Management groups sit above subscriptions, and policies and role assignments applied there are inherited by every subscription beneath. Resource groups sit below subscriptions.","Manage subscriptions and management groups",[null,"Resource groups sit inside a single subscription, so they cannot group whole subscriptions by department.","Administrative units scope Entra ID admin permissions over users, groups and devices; they do not hold Azure subscriptions or policies.","Blueprints are deprecated, and applying artifacts to each resource individually is the opposite of central, inherited assignment."],1],["adm13",0,1,"A team wants an email when their subscription's monthly spending forecast passes 80% of a set amount. What should be configured?",["An activity log alert on resource write operations in the subscription","A Cost Management budget with a forecasted alert threshold","An Azure Advisor recommendation digest","A resource lock on the subscription"],1,"Budgets in Cost Management can alert on actual or forecasted cost at thresholds such as 80%. Advisor digests summarize recommendations, not spending against a limit.","Manage costs",["Activity log alerts fire on operations such as writes or deletes, not on spending thresholds or forecasts.",null,"Advisor digests summarize recommendations on a schedule; they do not compare spending with an amount you set.","A lock controls delete or write operations and has no cost or notification function."],2],["adm14",0,1,"You must move a VM and its disks and NIC from RG-Dev to RG-Prod in the same subscription. What is true?",["The VM must be recreated from a snapshot in the new resource group","Resources keep their region; move dependent resources together","The move changes the VM's region to match RG-Prod","Moving resource groups is only possible with ARM templates"],1,"A resource group move changes only the group, not the region, and a VM should move with its dependent disks and NICs. Resources are locked for writes during the move, but nothing is recreated.","Manage resource groups",["Moving between resource groups does not recreate the VM; Azure moves the existing resources and their IDs change to reflect the new group.",null,"A resource's region is fixed when it is created; a resource group move never changes it, and the group's own location is only metadata.","You can move resources between groups from the portal, Azure CLI, PowerShell or the REST API; templates are not required."],2],["adm15",0,1,"Users want to reset their own forgotten passwords without calling the help desk. What should you enable?",["Self-service password reset for a group or all users","Conditional Access that requires a compliant or hybrid-joined device","Privileged Identity Management for all users","A password expiration policy of 30 days"],0,"Self-service password reset lets registered users reset their password with methods such as the Authenticator app or phone. Conditional Access and PIM do not reset passwords.","Configure self-service password reset",[null,"Conditional Access decides when and how users can sign in; it does not let users reset a forgotten password.","Privileged Identity Management provides just-in-time activation of privileged roles; it is not a password reset tool.","Forcing more frequent expiration makes forgotten passwords more likely and still leaves resets to the help desk."],1],["adm16",0,1,"You want every member of the Sales group to receive a Microsoft 365 license automatically, including future members. What should you do?",["Assign licenses to each current user through a scheduled PowerShell script","Add the Sales group as owner of the license","Create a budget scoped to the Sales group","Assign the license to the Sales group using group-based licensing"],3,"Group-based licensing assigns the license to everyone in the group and follows membership changes. A script only covers current members, and licenses have no owners.","Manage licenses",["A scheduled script only licenses users who exist at run time and adds maintenance; group-based licensing follows membership changes on its own.","Licenses do not have owners, so making a group an owner of a license is not a real configuration.","Budgets track Azure spending and cannot be scoped to an Entra group or assign licenses.",null],1],["adm17",0,1,"A developer needs to create 500 user accounts in Microsoft Entra ID from a spreadsheet. What is the simplest built-in method?",["Invite each user through B2B collaboration from a partner tenant","Create a dynamic group with 500 members","Use Bulk create with the downloaded CSV template","Create a management group for the new users"],2,"The Entra admin center offers Bulk create with a CSV template. B2B invites are for external users, and dynamic groups manage membership, not accounts.","Manage Entra users and groups",["B2B invitations create guest accounts for people from other organizations, not 500 new member accounts in your tenant.","A dynamic group only manages membership of accounts that already exist; it cannot create users.",null,"Management groups organize Azure subscriptions; they have nothing to do with creating Entra user accounts."],1],["adm18",0,1,"A policy definition needs to create a diagnostic setting on any Key Vault that lacks one. Which effect is required?",["Audit","DeployIfNotExists","Deny","Append"],1,"DeployIfNotExists deploys a related resource (such as a diagnostic setting) when it is missing. Audit only reports, Deny blocks the request and Append adds fields to the request.","Implement and manage Azure Policy",["Audit only marks resources as non-compliant; it never creates the missing diagnostic setting.",null,"Deny blocks non-compliant requests but cannot create a related resource on an existing Key Vault.","Append adds fields to the resource being created or updated; it cannot deploy a separate resource such as a diagnostic setting."],1],["adm19",0,1,"Several related policy definitions must be assigned together and tracked as one compliance score. What should you create?",["A management group","A policy exemption","A custom RBAC role","An initiative (policy set definition)"],3,"An initiative groups policy definitions into one assignment with a combined compliance view. An exemption removes resources from evaluation instead.","Implement and manage Azure Policy",["A management group is a scope where you assign policies, not a way to bundle definitions into one compliance score.","A policy exemption removes a scope from evaluation, which is the opposite of grouping definitions for tracking.","A custom RBAC role defines permissions for actions; it does not group policy definitions or report compliance.",null],1],["adm20",0,1,"Azure Advisor shows several VMs with very low CPU use over two weeks. Which kind of recommendation is this and what is the usual action?",["A cost recommendation to resize or shut down underused VMs","A security recommendation to enable just-in-time VM access on each VM","A reliability recommendation to add availability zones","An operational excellence recommendation to add tags"],0,"Advisor cost recommendations flag underused VMs so you can right-size or shut them down. Security, reliability and operational recommendations address different issues.","Manage costs",[null,"Just-in-time VM access is a Defender for Cloud control for management ports; it does not address low CPU use.","Availability zones improve resilience; they do not fix VMs that are oversized for their load.","Tags help organize resources, but low CPU use is flagged as a cost opportunity, not an operational excellence issue."],1],["adm21",0,1,"Which Entra ID group type can be used to assign Azure RBAC roles to its members?",["A Microsoft 365 group with dynamic device membership rules","Distribution list synchronized from Exchange","A security group or a security-enabled Microsoft 365 group","Any group type, including mail-enabled distribution lists"],2,"Role assignments need security principals: security groups, and security-enabled Microsoft 365 groups. Distribution lists are not security principals.","Manage Entra users and groups",["Microsoft 365 groups cannot use dynamic device membership at all (only security groups can), and what makes a group usable for RBAC is being security-enabled, not its rule.","Distribution lists are mail-only objects, not security principals, so they cannot receive Azure role assignments.",null,"Mail-enabled distribution lists are not security principals, so not every group type can be used for RBAC."],2],["adm22",0,1,"An admin gave a user Contributor on a subscription, but the user cannot see a resource in RG1 that has a deny assignment for that user. What explains this?",["Contributor does not include read permissions on resources","Deny assignments take priority over role assignments","The user needs the Owner role to override a deny assignment","Deny assignments only apply to Entra roles"],1,"Deny assignments block actions even when a role assignment allows them. Owner does not override them, and Contributor includes read permissions.","Interpret access assignments",["Contributor includes read permissions on all resources in its scope, so missing reads are not the cause.",null,"Owner does not override deny assignments; deny assignments block the listed actions even for Owners.","Deny assignments apply to Azure resource (RBAC) permissions, not to Entra ID directory roles."],3],["adm23",0,2,"A storage account must survive the loss of one datacenter within the region but data must never leave the region. Which redundancy should you choose?",["LRS","GRS","ZRS","RA-GRS"],2,"ZRS keeps three copies across availability zones in the same region. LRS stays in one datacenter, and GRS or RA-GRS copy data to a paired region.","Configure redundancy",["LRS keeps all three copies in a single datacenter, so losing that datacenter can make the data unavailable.","GRS copies data asynchronously to the paired region, which breaks the rule that data must never leave the region.",null,"RA-GRS also replicates to the paired region (and adds read access there), so data leaves the region."],2],["adm24",0,2,"An app must keep reading blob data from a secondary region while the primary region is unavailable, with zone resilience in the primary. Which option fits?",["RA-GZRS","GRS","ZRS","LRS"],0,"RA-GZRS uses zones in the primary region and lets clients read from the secondary endpoint. GRS copies to the secondary but reads there only after a failover.","Configure redundancy",[null,"GRS keeps the primary copies in one datacenter (no zones) and the secondary is readable only after a failover.","ZRS is zone-resilient but has no secondary region, so there is nothing to read from during a regional outage.","LRS has neither zone redundancy nor a secondary region."],2],["adm25",0,2,"A contractor needs to upload files to one container for the next 8 hours only. You want the most secure SAS that does not use the account key. Which should you issue?",["An account SAS with write permission for all services","A service SAS signed with key1","The storage account's connection string","A user delegation SAS scoped to the container"],3,"A user delegation SAS is signed with Entra credentials instead of an account key and can be limited to the container and 8 hours. A connection string gives full account access.","Configure access to storage",["An account SAS is signed with an account key and here grants write to every service, far broader than one container.","A service SAS signed with key1 depends on the account key, which the requirement says to avoid, and is less secure than a user delegation SAS.","A connection string contains the account key and grants full control of the whole account with no expiry.",null],3],["adm26",0,2,"You issued service SAS tokens for a container and must be able to revoke them all without rotating account keys. What should you have used?",["A stored access policy referenced by the tokens","An IP address range in each SAS token","A ReadOnly lock on the container","Blob versioning on the account"],0,"Service SAS tokens linked to a stored access policy can be revoked by changing or deleting the policy. Without it, only key rotation or expiry ends them.","Configure access to storage",[null,"An IP range limits where a token can be used from but gives you no way to revoke tokens that are already issued.","A lock protects the container resource on the management plane; it does not invalidate SAS tokens for data access.","Versioning keeps previous blob versions; it has no effect on whether a SAS token is still valid."],2],["adm27",0,2,"A storage account must be reachable only from one subnet in your VNet and from Azure Backup, with the public endpoint still in use. What should you configure?",["A private endpoint in the subnet with public network access disabled","Storage firewall VNet rule plus the trusted services exception","A network security group on the storage account","A SAS token restricted to the subnet's IP range"],1,"Storage firewall VNet rules (with a service endpoint on the subnet) limit the public endpoint to that subnet, and the trusted Microsoft services exception lets Azure Backup through. NSGs cannot attach to storage accounts.","Configure storage firewalls and virtual networks",["A private endpoint with public access disabled removes the public endpoint, but the requirement says the public endpoint stays in use.",null,"NSGs attach to subnets and NICs, not to storage accounts, so one cannot filter access to the account.","SAS tokens can restrict public IP ranges, but VNet traffic reaches storage from private addresses, and a SAS does not stop other clients from using keys."],3],["adm28",0,2,"Security requires that storage encryption keys be controlled and rotatable by the company. What should you configure?",["Infrastructure encryption only","Microsoft-managed keys with key rotation","Customer-managed keys stored in Azure Key Vault","An encryption scope that uses Microsoft-managed keys"],2,"Customer-managed keys in Key Vault or Managed HSM put key control and rotation with the customer. Microsoft-managed keys are rotated by Microsoft.","Configure encryption",["Infrastructure encryption adds a second layer with Microsoft-managed keys; it does not give the company control of the keys.","With Microsoft-managed keys Microsoft owns and rotates the keys, so the company does not control them.",null,"An encryption scope that uses Microsoft-managed keys still leaves key control with Microsoft."],1],["adm29",0,2,"A team wants double encryption at rest for a new storage account. When must infrastructure encryption be enabled?",["At any time from the Encryption blade","Only after customer-managed keys are set up","After the account is converted to GRS","When the storage account is created"],3,"Infrastructure encryption must be enabled when the account is created and cannot be turned on later. It does not depend on redundancy or on customer-managed keys.","Configure encryption",["Infrastructure encryption cannot be switched on later from the Encryption blade; it is fixed when the account is created.","Infrastructure encryption does not depend on customer-managed keys and must be chosen at creation, not afterwards.","Changing redundancy has nothing to do with infrastructure encryption, which can only be set at creation.",null],2],["adm30",0,2,"Log files are read often for 30 days, rarely for a year, then must be deleted. How can you manage this automatically at the lowest cost?",["A lifecycle rule: Cool after 30 days, delete after 365 days","A scheduled Azure Function that copies old blobs to a second account","Setting the account default tier to Archive","Enabling blob soft delete with a 365-day retention"],0,"Lifecycle management tiers and deletes blobs by age with no code. Archive as a default is not supported at account level, and soft delete keeps deleted data rather than removing it.","Configure Blob Storage",[null,"A custom Function adds code to maintain and a second account to pay for, while lifecycle management does the same with no code.","Archive cannot be the account's default tier, and archived logs would be offline during the 30 days they are read often.","Soft delete keeps deleted blobs recoverable; it does not delete old data or move it to cheaper tiers."],2],["adm31",0,2,"A blob in the Archive tier is needed urgently for an audit. What must happen before it can be read?",["Nothing; Archive blobs can be read directly, only with higher latency","It must be rehydrated to an online tier such as Hot or Cool","It must be copied with AzCopy to a file share","The account must be converted to Premium"],1,"Archive is offline storage. The blob must be rehydrated to Hot, Cool or Cold (or copied to an online blob), which can take hours; high priority can be faster for small blobs.","Configure Blob Storage",["Archive blobs are offline, so they cannot be read at all until they are rehydrated or copied to an online tier.",null,"AzCopy cannot read an archived blob's data either, and copying to Azure Files is not how rehydration works.","Premium performance is a separate account type; converting the account does not bring archived blobs online."],1],["adm32",0,2,"You must replicate block blobs from a container in one account to a container in another region's account asynchronously. What must be enabled first?",["Soft delete and point-in-time restore on both accounts","Hierarchical namespace on the destination account","Premium performance on the source","Versioning on both accounts, change feed on the source"],3,"Object replication requires versioning on both source and destination and change feed on the source. Hierarchical namespace accounts are not supported for object replication.","Configure object replication",["Soft delete and point-in-time restore protect data within an account; they are not prerequisites for object replication.","Accounts with hierarchical namespace are not supported for object replication, so enabling it would block the setup.","Object replication works with standard general-purpose v2 accounts; Premium performance is not required.",null],3],["adm33",0,2,"A user accidentally overwrote an important blob. Which feature would let you restore the previous content?",["Container soft delete","Blob versioning","Storage firewall","Access tier set to Cool"],1,"Versioning keeps previous versions each time a blob is overwritten. Container soft delete protects against deleting a whole container, not overwrites.","Configure data protection",["Container soft delete restores a whole deleted container, not the previous content of a blob that was overwritten.",null,"The storage firewall controls which networks can reach the account and cannot recover overwritten data.","The Cool tier lowers storage cost but keeps no earlier copies of a blob."],1],["adm34",0,2,"You need to copy 2 TB of files from an on-premises server to Blob Storage with a scriptable, resumable command-line tool. What should you use?",["AzCopy","Azure Storage Explorer's drag and drop","The portal upload blade","Robocopy to an SMB share"],0,"AzCopy is the command-line tool for high-throughput, resumable copies to and between storage accounts. Portal uploads and drag-and-drop do not suit terabytes or automation.","Manage data with AzCopy and Storage Explorer",[null,"Storage Explorer's drag and drop is interactive, not scriptable, and is poorly suited to 2 TB automated copies.","Portal uploads are meant for small files and cannot be scripted or resumed for terabytes of data.","Robocopy copies to SMB file shares, not to Blob Storage."],1],["adm35",0,2,"Users in a branch office cannot mount an Azure file share, although it works from Azure VMs. What is the most likely cause?",["The share is using the Hot tier","The storage account uses ZRS","The branch ISP blocks outbound TCP 445","Share snapshots are enabled"],2,"SMB uses TCP 445, which many ISPs and networks block outbound. Tier, redundancy and snapshots do not stop mounting.","Configure Azure Files",["Access tiers affect cost and performance, not whether a client can open an SMB connection to the share.","ZRS only controls how data is replicated inside Azure and does not affect mounting from a branch office.",null,"Share snapshots are read-only point-in-time copies and do not stop clients from mounting the share."],2],["adm36",0,2,"Domain users should access an Azure file share with their AD credentials and NTFS permissions. What is the correct order of work?",["Assign Reader on the storage account and share the account key","Create a SAS token per user and map it as a network drive","Enable large file shares and set only NTFS permissions on the share root","Enable identity-based auth, assign share roles, then set NTFS ACLs"],3,"Identity-based access needs an identity source (AD DS, Entra Domain Services or Entra Kerberos), share-level roles such as Storage File Data SMB Share Contributor, then Windows ACLs. The account key bypasses identity.","Configure identity-based access for Azure Files",["Reader on the account plus the account key gives everyone full, shared access and bypasses AD credentials and NTFS permissions.","SAS tokens cannot be used to mount an SMB file share and do not carry the user's AD identity.","Large file shares only raise capacity limits, and NTFS ACLs alone do nothing until identity-based auth and share-level roles are in place.",null],3],["adm37",0,2,"A developer deleted a container by mistake. Which setting, if enabled beforehand, allows it to be restored?",["Container soft delete","Blob snapshots","Change feed","Lifecycle management"],0,"Container soft delete retains deleted containers for the retention period. Blob snapshots protect individual blobs, and change feed only logs changes.","Configure data protection",[null,"Blob snapshots protect individual blobs, and they are lost along with the container when it is deleted.","Change feed records a log of changes; it does not keep deleted containers.","Lifecycle management moves or deletes blobs by age; it cannot restore anything."],1],["adm38",0,2,"A security review found an access key was leaked. What should you do to invalidate it while keeping apps running?",["Enable soft delete and wait for the leaked key to expire on its own","Switch apps to the other key, then regenerate the leaked key","Delete the storage account and restore it","Change the account's redundancy to GRS"],1,"Two keys exist so you can move apps to the unaffected key and then regenerate the leaked one. Keys do not expire, and redundancy is unrelated.","Manage access keys",["Storage account keys never expire on their own, so waiting leaves the leaked key usable; soft delete protects data, not keys.",null,"Deleting the account destroys the data and every app connection, which is drastic and does not keep apps running.","Redundancy settings control replication and have no effect on which keys are valid."],2],["adm39",0,2,"Different departments share one storage account but each must use its own customer-managed key for its containers. What should you use?",["Separate access tiers per container","Stored access policies","Encryption scopes","Object replication rules"],2,"Encryption scopes let you choose a key per container or blob in the same account. Stored access policies control SAS, not encryption.","Configure encryption",["Access tiers set cost and availability for blobs; they have nothing to do with which encryption key is used.","Stored access policies control SAS tokens, not encryption keys.",null,"Object replication copies blobs to another account; it does not assign encryption keys per container."],3],["adm40",0,3,"You redeploy an ARM template in complete mode to a resource group that contains an extra storage account not in the template. What happens to that account?",["It is deleted","It is left unchanged","It is locked as ReadOnly","It is moved to a new resource group"],0,"Complete mode deletes resources in the resource group that are not defined in the template. Incremental mode, the default, would leave it alone.","Automate deployment with ARM templates or Bicep",[null,"Left unchanged is what incremental mode (the default) does; complete mode removes resources that are not in the template.","Deployments never apply locks unless the template defines one; complete mode deletes extra resources instead.","Deployments do not move resources between resource groups; moves are a separate operation."],2],["adm41",0,3,"An admin has an existing ARM JSON template and wants to maintain it as Bicep from now on. Which command helps?",["az bicep build","az bicep decompile","az deployment group export","az group create --template"],1,"`az bicep decompile` converts ARM JSON to a Bicep file, which you then review. `az bicep build` goes the other way, from Bicep to JSON.","Convert an ARM template to Bicep",["az bicep build compiles Bicep into ARM JSON, the opposite of what is needed.",null,"Export produces a template from existing resources, not a Bicep version of your existing JSON file.","az group create creates a resource group and does not take a template or convert one."],1],["adm42",0,3,"A resource group was built by hand in the portal, and you need a template to recreate it in another subscription. What is the quickest starting point?",["Create a new blank template in VS Code","Copy the activity log to a JSON file","Use Export template on the resource group","Run az deployment group what-if"],2,"Export template generates an ARM template (and Bicep in the portal) from existing resources, which you then clean up and parameterize. What-if only previews a deployment.","Export a deployment as a template",["Writing a template from scratch is slow and easy to get wrong when the resources already exist to export from.","The activity log records operations, not a deployable description of the resources.",null,"What-if previews the effect of a template you already have; it does not generate one."],1],["adm43",0,3,"A Bicep file must use a different VM size in dev and prod without editing the file. What should you add?",["A variable with a fixed size value","An output that returns the VM size after the deployment finishes","A resource dependency on the VNet","A parameter for the VM size, set per environment"],3,"Parameters take values at deployment time, for example from a parameters file per environment. Variables are computed inside the file, and outputs return values after deployment.","Interpret and modify templates",["A variable is computed inside the file, so changing the size would still mean editing the file for each environment.","Outputs return values after deployment finishes; they cannot feed a different size into the deployment.","A dependency only controls deployment order and does not change the VM size.",null],1],["adm44",0,3,"Before running a template against production, you want to see which resources would be created, changed or deleted. What should you use?",["The what-if operation","Validate-only mode in Storage Explorer","The Activity log","An Azure Policy Audit effect"],0,"`az deployment group what-if` (or -WhatIf in PowerShell) previews changes without deploying. The activity log only shows what already happened.","Deploy resources by using templates",[null,"Storage Explorer manages storage data and has no validate-only mode for ARM or Bicep deployments.","The activity log only shows operations that already happened, not what a deployment would change.","An Audit policy reports compliance of resources; it does not preview a template's creates, changes and deletes."],1],["adm45",0,3,"A two-VM web tier must stay available if one entire datacenter in the region fails. What should you deploy?",["Both VMs in one availability set","Both VMs in separate availability zones","Both VMs on a dedicated host","One VM with Premium SSD disks"],1,"Availability zones are physically separate datacenters, so VMs in different zones survive a datacenter outage (99.99% SLA). Availability sets only spread VMs across racks in one datacenter.","Configure VM availability",["An availability set spreads VMs across racks in one datacenter, so losing that datacenter takes down both VMs.",null,"A dedicated host is a single physical server, which is a single point of failure rather than protection.","Premium SSD improves disk performance and the single-VM SLA, but one VM cannot survive the loss of its datacenter."],2],["adm46",0,3,"VMs in an availability set are spread across fault domains and update domains. What do update domains protect against?",["Loss of power or networking to a single server rack","Loss of an entire region","Planned maintenance rebooting all VMs at once","Accidental deletion of a VM"],2,"Update domains ensure planned maintenance reboots only one group at a time. Fault domains protect against rack-level hardware, power or network failure.","Configure VM availability",["Protection against a rack losing power or networking is what fault domains provide, not update domains.","Neither update domains nor fault domains protect against a region outage; that needs a second region.",null,"Update domains group VMs for maintenance; they do nothing to prevent someone deleting a VM."],1],["adm47",0,3,"You try to resize a running VM, but the size you need is not listed. What should you do?",["Delete the VM and recreate it from the marketplace","Change the OS disk to Ultra disk","Move the VM to another resource group","Stop (deallocate) the VM and check the sizes again"],3,"If a size is not available on the current hardware cluster, deallocating the VM lets Azure place it on hardware that supports the new size.","Manage VM sizes",["Recreating the VM is unnecessary, because deallocating it usually exposes the sizes supported on other hardware.","Ultra Disk is a data disk type that cannot be an OS disk, and disk type does not decide which VM sizes are listed.","Resource group membership does not affect which sizes the hardware cluster supports.",null],2],["adm48",0,3,"A VM's temporary disk, OS disk and caches must be encrypted end to end on the host, without running BitLocker or DM-Crypt inside the guest. What should you enable?",["Encryption at host","Azure Disk Encryption","A storage account firewall","Trusted launch only"],0,"Encryption at host encrypts temp disks and caches at the VM host, with no in-guest agent. Azure Disk Encryption uses BitLocker or DM-Crypt inside the OS.","Configure VM disk encryption",[null,"Azure Disk Encryption runs BitLocker or DM-Crypt inside the guest OS, which the requirement rules out.","A storage account firewall controls network access to storage; it does not encrypt VM disks or caches.","Trusted launch adds secure boot and vTPM protections; it does not encrypt temp disks and caches on the host."],3],["adm49",0,3,"A database VM needs the lowest disk latency and settings for IOPS and throughput that can be changed on the fly, for its data disk. Which disk type fits best?",["Standard HDD","Ultra Disk or Premium SSD v2","Standard SSD","An ephemeral OS disk"],1,"Ultra Disk and Premium SSD v2 offer sub-millisecond latency and adjustable IOPS and throughput for data disks. Neither can be used as an OS disk; ephemeral OS disks are lost on deallocation.","Manage VM disks",["Standard HDD has the highest latency and lowest performance, and its IOPS cannot be tuned on the fly.",null,"Standard SSD is more consistent than HDD but does not offer sub-millisecond latency or adjustable IOPS and throughput.","Ephemeral OS disks hold only the OS on local storage, are lost on deallocation and cannot serve as a database data disk."],2],["adm50",0,3,"A VM must move from West US to North Europe with minimal manual rebuild. Which approach is supported?",["Change the region field in the VM's properties","Move the VM into a resource group whose location is North Europe","Use Azure Resource Mover or Site Recovery to move it","Detach the NIC and attach it to a North Europe VNet"],2,"Cross-region moves need Resource Mover or Site Recovery replication. Moving to a resource group in another region does not change the VM's region.","Move VMs",["A VM's region is fixed at creation and is not an editable property.","A resource group's location only stores its metadata; moving a VM into it does not change the VM's region.",null,"A NIC must be in the same region as its VM, so it cannot be attached to a VNet in another region to move the VM."],2],["adm51",0,3,"A web tier must add VM instances automatically when average CPU exceeds 70% and remove them when it drops. What should you deploy?",["An availability set with 10 VMs spread across fault domains","A single larger VM size","A proximity placement group","A Virtual Machine Scale Set with autoscale rules"],3,"Scale sets with autoscale add and remove instances based on metrics such as CPU. An availability set does not scale automatically.","Deploy and configure scale sets",["An availability set improves resilience, but it never adds or removes VMs based on CPU load.","A single larger VM still has a fixed capacity and does not remove instances when load drops.","A proximity placement group reduces latency between VMs by placing them close together; it does not scale anything.",null],1],["adm52",0,3,"A company needs a private container registry replicated to three regions with private endpoints. Which ACR tier is required?",["Premium","Basic","Standard","Any tier with admin user enabled"],0,"Geo-replication and private link are Premium features. Basic and Standard differ mainly in storage and throughput.","Manage Azure Container Registry",[null,"Basic does not support geo-replication or private endpoints.","Standard offers more storage and throughput than Basic but still lacks geo-replication and private link.","The admin user is a single shared login and has no effect on which features a tier supports."],2],["adm53",0,3,"A nightly batch job runs in one container for 20 minutes and must not restart after it exits successfully. What is the simplest option?",["An App Service plan with Always On","Azure Container Instances with restart policy OnFailure","A Virtual Machine Scale Set with one instance","Azure Container Apps with the minimum replica count set to 3"],1,"ACI runs a container group on demand, and OnFailure restarts only if the container exits with an error. The other options keep compute running permanently.","Provision Azure Container Instances",["An App Service plan with Always On keeps compute running and billed all day for a 20-minute job.",null,"A scale set keeps at least one VM running, so you pay for idle compute and must manage the VM.","Container Apps with a minimum of three replicas keeps three copies running all the time, the opposite of a short batch run."],3],["adm54",0,3,"A microservice should scale from zero to many replicas based on HTTP traffic and split traffic between two versions. Which service fits?",["Azure Container Instances","A single VM running Docker","Azure Container Apps","Azure Container Registry Tasks"],2,"Container Apps provides revisions with traffic splitting and KEDA-based scaling, including scale to zero. ACI has no built-in autoscaling or revisions.","Manage Azure Container Apps",["Container Instances run container groups on demand but have no built-in autoscaling or revision traffic splitting.","A single Docker VM needs you to build scaling and traffic splitting yourself and cannot scale to zero.",null,"ACR Tasks build and patch container images; they do not run or scale a microservice."],2],["adm55",0,3,"An App Service app needs deployment slots so new versions can be tested and swapped into production. What is the minimum plan tier?",["Free","Shared","Basic","Standard"],3,"Deployment slots start at the Standard tier (Premium and Isolated offer more slots). Free, Shared and Basic have no slots.","Configure deployment slots",["The Free tier is for testing and has no deployment slots.","The Shared tier runs apps on shared compute and has no deployment slots.","Basic adds dedicated compute and custom domains but still has no deployment slots.",null],1],["adm56",0,3,"A web app's App Service plan is at 90% memory and you need a larger instance size. Which action is this?",["Scale up the App Service plan","Scale out to more instances","Add a deployment slot","Enable VNet integration"],0,"Scaling up moves the plan to a bigger size or tier with more memory and CPU. Scaling out adds more instances of the same size.","Configure scaling for App Service",[null,"Scaling out adds more instances of the same size, which does not give each instance more memory.","A deployment slot is a separate app instance for staging and does not add memory to production.","VNet integration provides outbound access to a virtual network and does not change instance size."],1],["adm57",0,3,"After swapping staging into production, the production app started using the staging database connection string. How do you prevent this next time?",["Store the connection string directly in the application code","Mark the connection string as a deployment slot setting","Use a Free tier plan for staging","Disable autoswap on production"],1,"Slot settings stick to their slot and do not move during a swap. Normal app settings and connection strings swap with the content.","Configure deployment slots",["Hard-coding the connection string would travel with the code during a swap and makes secrets harder to manage.",null,"The Free tier has no deployment slots at all, and the plan tier has nothing to do with which settings swap.","Autoswap only triggers the swap automatically; a manual swap would still move the staging connection string."],3],["adm58",0,3,"You are mapping www.contoso.com to an App Service app. Which DNS record is typically created for this subdomain?",["An MX record pointing to the app","A PTR record for the app's IP","A CNAME record to the app's azurewebsites.net name","An NS record pointing to the App Service name servers for the app"],2,"A subdomain such as www usually maps with a CNAME to the default host name (plus a TXT asuid record for verification). MX is for mail and PTR for reverse lookups.","Map a custom DNS name",["MX records route email for a domain and cannot point a web host name to an app.","PTR records are for reverse lookups from an IP address to a name, not for mapping a host name to an app.",null,"NS records delegate a whole zone to name servers; App Service does not host DNS zones for individual apps."],1],["adm59",0,3,"A web app must call a database that is reachable only on a private IP inside a VNet. Which App Service feature provides this outbound access?",["A private endpoint on the web app","A custom domain with TLS","Access restrictions","VNet integration"],3,"VNet integration lets the app make outbound calls into the VNet. A private endpoint gives inbound private access to the app, and access restrictions filter inbound traffic.","Configure networking for App Service",["A private endpoint gives clients inbound private access to the app; it does not let the app call into the VNet.","A custom domain with TLS changes how users reach the app and has no effect on its outbound connectivity.","Access restrictions filter inbound traffic to the app; they do not provide outbound access to private IPs.",null],2],["adm60",0,3,"An App Service app needs automatic scheduled backups of its content and configuration. What must be true?",["The plan tier must support backups (Basic or higher)","The app must run in a container","A Recovery Services vault must be in the same region","The app must have a deployment slot"],0,"App Service backups are built into supported plan tiers (Basic and above) and store data in a storage account. They do not use a Recovery Services vault or require containers or slots.","Configure backup for App Service",[null,"App Service backups work for code-based apps too; running in a container is not required.","App Service backups are stored in a storage account, not a Recovery Services vault.","Backups do not depend on deployment slots; a single production app can be backed up."],2],["adm61",0,3,"A scale set runs a stateless app. During scale-in, you want the newest VMs removed first. What should you configure?",["An availability set","A scale-in policy of NewestVM","Manual scaling only","A proximity placement group"],1,"Scale-in policies (Default, NewestVM, OldestVM) choose which instances are removed. Availability sets and placement groups do not control scale-in order.","Deploy and configure scale sets",["Availability sets place VMs across fault and update domains; they do not decide which scale set instances are removed.",null,"Manual scaling only changes who decides the instance count; it does not choose the newest VMs to remove.","A proximity placement group controls physical placement for low latency, not scale-in order."],2],["adm62",0,4,"You are planning a subnet for 27 VMs and want to use as little address space as possible. What is the smallest subnet that fits?",["/28","/26","/27","/29"],2,"Azure reserves 5 addresses in every subnet. A /27 has 32 addresses, leaving exactly 27 usable. A /28 leaves only 11, and a /26 fits but wastes space.","Configure virtual networks",["A /28 has 16 addresses, and after Azure reserves 5 only 11 remain, which is too few for 27 VMs.","A /26 leaves 59 usable addresses, so it fits but wastes space compared with a /27.",null,"A /29 has 8 addresses and only 3 usable after Azure's reservations."],2],["adm63",0,4,"VNet A is peered with VNet B, and VNet B with VNet C. VMs in A cannot reach VMs in C. What is the reason?",["Global peering is required between regions","NSGs block all peered traffic by default","VNet peering is not transitive","The address spaces must be the same"],2,"Peering is non-transitive, so A needs its own peering with C, or traffic must go through an NVA or gateway in B with user-defined routes. Default NSG rules allow VirtualNetwork traffic.","Configure VNet peering",["Global peering connects VNets in different regions, but it is still not transitive, and the question does not say the VNets are in different regions.","The default AllowVnetInBound rule permits traffic between peered VNets, so NSGs are not blocking it by default.",null,"Peered VNets must have non-overlapping address spaces, so identical address spaces would prevent peering."],1],["adm64",0,4,"Spoke VNets must use the hub VNet's VPN gateway to reach on-premises. Which peering settings are needed?",["Allow gateway transit on the hub, Use remote gateways on the spokes","Use remote gateways on the hub, Allow gateway transit on the spokes","Allow forwarded traffic only on both sides","A second VPN gateway in each spoke"],0,"The hub peering allows gateway transit and each spoke peering uses remote gateways. Reversing them does not work, and a spoke with remote gateways cannot have its own gateway.","Configure VNet peering",[null,"This reverses the settings: the hub must allow gateway transit and the spokes must use the remote gateway.","Allow forwarded traffic permits traffic that did not originate in the peered VNet, but it does not let spokes use the hub's gateway.","A gateway in each spoke is costly and unnecessary, and a spoke that uses remote gateways cannot have its own gateway."],3],["adm65",0,4,"All internet-bound traffic from a subnet must pass through a firewall appliance at 10.0.1.4. What should you create?",["An NSG outbound rule whose destination is 10.0.1.4","A UDR for 0.0.0.0/0 with next hop Virtual appliance","A service endpoint for Microsoft.Network on the subnet","A private DNS zone for the firewall"],1,"A user-defined route with 0.0.0.0/0 and next hop type Virtual appliance sends traffic to the NVA. NSGs only allow or deny traffic; they cannot redirect it.","Configure user-defined routes",["NSG rules only allow or deny traffic; they cannot change the next hop to send traffic through a firewall.",null,"Service endpoints route traffic to specific Azure services over the backbone; there is no endpoint that forces traffic through an appliance.","A private DNS zone only resolves names and cannot change how packets are routed."],2],["adm66",0,4,"An NSG has an inbound rule allowing TCP 3389 at priority 300 and a rule denying TCP 3389 at priority 200. What happens to RDP traffic?",["It is allowed, because allow rules always win over deny rules","It is allowed, because 300 is higher","It depends on the NIC NSG only","It is denied, because priority 200 is processed first"],3,"Lower numbers are processed first and processing stops at the first match, so the priority-200 deny applies.","Configure network security groups",["NSGs do not favor allow rules; the rule with the lowest priority number that matches wins.","A higher priority number means lower precedence, so 300 is processed after 200.","The question concerns rules in one NSG, whose priority order decides the result regardless of any NIC NSG.",null],2],["adm67",0,4,"An NSG on a subnet allows inbound HTTPS, but a separate NSG on a VM's NIC has only the default rules. Can internet clients reach HTTPS on the VM?",["No, because the NIC NSG's default DenyAllInBound blocks it","Yes, because the subnet NSG overrides the NIC NSG","Yes, because default rules allow internet traffic","No, because two NSGs cannot be combined"],0,"Inbound traffic must be allowed by both the subnet NSG and the NIC NSG. The NIC's default rules deny inbound internet traffic.","Configure network security groups",[null,"Neither NSG overrides the other; inbound traffic must be allowed by both the subnet NSG and the NIC NSG.","The default rules allow VirtualNetwork and AzureLoadBalancer traffic but deny inbound internet traffic.","Subnet and NIC NSGs can be combined; traffic is evaluated against both."],3],["adm68",0,4,"Web servers and database servers share a subnet. You want NSG rules that refer to 'web' and 'db' roles instead of IP addresses. What should you use?",["Service tags","Route tables","Application security groups","Private DNS zones"],2,"Application security groups let you group NICs by role and use those groups as sources and destinations in NSG rules. Service tags represent Azure services, not your VMs.","Configure application security groups",["Service tags represent groups of Azure service IP ranges, such as Storage or AzureLoadBalancer, not your own VMs by role.","Route tables control where traffic goes next; they cannot group VMs for NSG rules.",null,"Private DNS zones resolve names inside VNets and cannot be used as NSG sources or destinations."],2],["adm69",0,4,"Admins need RDP and SSH to VMs that have no public IPs, from a browser over TLS. What should you deploy?",["A Standard Load Balancer with inbound NAT rules for RDP and SSH","A public IP on each VM with an NSG","A point-to-site VPN for each admin","Azure Bastion in a subnet named AzureBastionSubnet"],3,"Azure Bastion provides browser-based RDP and SSH over TLS and must be deployed into AzureBastionSubnet. Public IPs expose the VMs directly.","Configure Azure Bastion",["Inbound NAT rules on a load balancer still expose RDP and SSH ports to the internet and do not provide browser-based access over TLS.","Public IPs expose each VM's management ports directly, which the requirement for no public IPs rules out.","A point-to-site VPN gives network access but needs a client and gateway setup per admin, not browser-based RDP and SSH.",null],2],["adm70",0,4,"On-premises users connected by VPN must reach a storage account on a private IP, and public access must be disabled. What should you use?",["A service endpoint on the subnet","A private endpoint with a private DNS zone","A storage firewall IP rule for the VPN range","A user-defined route to the storage public IP"],1,"A private endpoint gives the storage account a private IP in the VNet that on-premises clients can reach over VPN, with private DNS for the privatelink name. Service endpoints only work from the VNet's own subnets.","Configure private endpoints",["Service endpoints only work for traffic from subnets in the VNet, not for on-premises clients over VPN, and still use the public endpoint.",null,"An IP rule for the VPN range still relies on the public endpoint, which must be disabled.","Routing to the storage public IP still uses the public endpoint, which contradicts disabling public access."],3],["adm71",0,4,"VMs in a VNet should get DNS names like vm1.corp.internal automatically as they are created. What should you configure?",["A public DNS zone with alias records for each VM","A CNAME record for each VM","A private DNS zone linked with auto-registration","A custom DNS server installed on each VM in the VNet"],2,"Private DNS zones linked with auto-registration create and remove A records for VMs in the VNet. Public zones are for internet names.","Configure Azure DNS",["Public DNS zones hold internet-facing names; they cannot auto-register VMs from a VNet.","Creating a CNAME for each VM is manual work and cannot happen automatically as VMs are created.",null,"Running a custom DNS server on every VM is unnecessary and would not register the VMs' names by itself."],2],["adm72",0,4,"You bought contoso.com at a third-party registrar and created a public zone in Azure DNS. Names still do not resolve through Azure. What is missing?",["An A record for the zone apex that points to your web server","A private DNS zone link","A reverse lookup zone","Registrar NS records pointing to Azure DNS"],3,"Delegation requires the registrar's NS records to point to the Azure DNS name servers shown on the zone. Records in the zone are ignored until then.","Configure Azure DNS",["An apex A record answers queries only after the zone is delegated; until the registrar points to Azure DNS, queries never reach the zone.","A private DNS zone link resolves names inside a VNet and has no effect on public internet resolution.","Reverse lookup zones map IP addresses to names and do not fix forward resolution of contoso.com.",null],2],["adm73",0,4,"An internal line-of-business app on three VMs needs a single private IP that spreads TCP traffic and skips unhealthy VMs. What should you deploy?",["An internal Standard Load Balancer with a health probe","A public Basic Load Balancer with a floating IP on each VM","Azure DNS round robin","A NAT gateway"],0,"An internal load balancer uses a private frontend IP, and health probes remove failed backends. DNS round robin has no health checks, and a NAT gateway handles outbound traffic only.","Configure an internal load balancer",[null,"A public load balancer exposes a public IP, which is wrong for an internal app, and the Basic SKU is retired.","DNS round robin has no health checks, so clients keep getting the addresses of unhealthy VMs.","A NAT gateway provides outbound internet access for a subnet and does not distribute inbound traffic."],2],["adm74",0,4,"Backend VMs behind a Standard Load Balancer are running, but clients get no response. The health probe shows all backends down. What should you check first?",["The load balancer's SKU","NSGs allow the AzureLoadBalancer probe and the port listens","The public DNS zone's TTL for the frontend record name","Whether the VMs have availability set membership"],1,"Health probes come from the AzureLoadBalancer service tag and must reach a listening port. If NSGs block them or the app is not listening, all backends are marked down.","Troubleshoot load balancing",["The SKU is already Standard, and changing it would not explain why every health probe is failing.",null,"DNS TTL affects how long clients cache the frontend address; it has no effect on backend health probes.","Availability set membership does not decide whether health probes succeed."],3],["adm75",0,4,"A VM cannot reach another VM on TCP 1433. You want to know which NSG rule is dropping the traffic. Which Network Watcher tool is quickest?",["Packet capture on both VMs","Connection Monitor dashboards","IP flow verify","Traffic analytics"],2,"IP flow verify tests a specific 5-tuple and names the NSG rule that allows or denies it. Packet capture shows traffic but not which rule dropped it.","Troubleshoot network connectivity",["Packet capture records traffic but does not name the NSG rule that dropped it, and it takes longer to set up and analyze.","Connection Monitor tracks reachability and latency over time; it is not the quickest way to identify a specific NSG rule.",null,"Traffic analytics summarizes flow logs over time and needs flow logging configured first, so it is not a quick per-flow check."],2],["adm76",0,4,"Traffic from a VM is not reaching the expected firewall. Which tool shows the route and next hop Azure will use for a destination?",["IP flow verify","Azure Advisor","Activity log","Network Watcher next hop"],3,"Next hop reports the next hop type and IP and the route table in effect. IP flow verify checks NSG rules, not routing.","Troubleshoot network connectivity",["IP flow verify checks whether NSG rules allow or deny a flow; it does not report routes or next hops.","Azure Advisor gives best-practice recommendations, not the route in effect for a destination.","The activity log records control-plane operations and does not show packet routing.",null],2],["adm77",0,4,"You need a public IP that is zone-redundant, static and closed to inbound traffic unless an NSG allows it. Which SKU fits?",["Standard","Basic","Dynamic Basic","Any SKU with a DNS label"],0,"Standard public IPs are always static, can be zone-redundant and are secure by default. The Basic SKU is retired and lacked zone redundancy.","Configure public IP addresses",[null,"The Basic SKU is retired, was open by default and did not support zone redundancy.","Dynamic Basic addresses change when the resource is deallocated, so they are neither static nor zone-redundant.","A DNS label only adds a name to the IP and does not make it static, zone-redundant or closed by default."],1],["adm78",0,4,"A storage account should accept traffic only from one subnet, and the storage account can keep its public endpoint. What is the simplest option?",["A private endpoint for the storage account in the subnet","A service endpoint plus a storage firewall VNet rule","A user-defined route with next hop None","An NSG with the Storage service tag on the account"],1,"Service endpoints with a VNet rule restrict the public endpoint to the subnet, which is simpler and cheaper than a private endpoint. NSGs cannot be attached to storage accounts.","Configure service endpoints",["A private endpoint works, but it adds cost and DNS setup, while the question asks for the simplest option with the public endpoint kept.",null,"A route with next hop None drops traffic; it cannot allow a subnet and block everyone else at the storage account.","NSGs attach to subnets and NICs, not storage accounts, so they cannot filter who reaches the account."],3],["adm79",0,5,"An admin wants a platform-level record of who deleted a resource group last week. Where should they look first?",["VM insights","The Activity log","Storage analytics logs","Connection Monitor"],1,"The Activity log records control-plane operations such as deletes, with the caller, for 90 days. VM insights and Connection Monitor focus on performance and connectivity.","Monitor resources in Azure",["VM insights shows performance and dependency data for VMs, not who deleted a resource group.",null,"Storage analytics logs record data-plane requests to a storage account, not resource group deletions.","Connection Monitor tracks network reachability and latency and records no management operations."],1],["adm80",0,5,"Resource logs from a Key Vault must be kept and queried with KQL alongside other logs. What should you configure?",["A diagnostic setting to a Log Analytics workspace","An action group with an email receiver for the vault owners","A metric alert on vault availability","A resource lock on the Key Vault"],0,"Resource logs are only collected when a diagnostic setting routes them to a destination such as Log Analytics. Alerts and action groups do not store logs.","Configure log settings",[null,"An action group defines who is notified when an alert fires; it does not collect or store logs.","A metric alert watches a metric value and neither collects resource logs nor lets you query them with KQL.","A lock prevents changes or deletion of the vault and has no effect on log collection."],2],["adm81",0,5,"Which KQL query returns the count of heartbeats per computer over the last hour?",["Heartbeat | project Computer, TimeGenerated | take 1h","Heartbeat | render timechart by Computer","Heartbeat | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) | summarize count() by Computer","Heartbeat | sort by Computer asc, TimeGenerated desc | limit 1h hours"],2,"`where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)` filters the time range and `summarize count() by Computer` groups the results. take and limit expect a number of rows, not a time.","Query and analyze logs",["take expects a number of rows, not a time span, and project alone does not count heartbeats per computer.","render only chooses a chart type and does not filter to the last hour or count rows.",null,"limit expects a row count, so '1h hours' is invalid, and sorting does not count heartbeats."],2],["adm82",0,5,"Alerts should keep firing during a planned weekend maintenance window, but nobody should be notified. What should you create?",["A new action group with no receivers attached to every alert rule","A ReadOnly lock on the alert rules","A budget alert","An alert processing rule that suppresses notifications"],3,"Alert processing rules can suppress action groups for a schedule while alerts still fire and are recorded. Editing action groups or locking rules is clumsy and error-prone.","Configure alert processing rules",["An empty action group attached to every rule is error-prone and must be undone later; alert processing rules handle schedules cleanly.","A ReadOnly lock stops edits to the alert rules but does not stop notifications from being sent.","A budget alert watches spending and has no effect on other alerts' notifications.",null],3],["adm83",0,5,"When a VM's CPU alert fires, the on-call team should get an SMS and a runbook should restart a service. Where are these responses defined?",["In an action group","In the diagnostic setting","In the Log Analytics workspace","In a data collection rule"],0,"Action groups hold notification and action receivers such as SMS, email, webhooks and runbooks. Diagnostic settings and DCRs control data collection.","Configure alerts and action groups",[null,"A diagnostic setting routes logs and metrics to a destination; it does not send SMS or run runbooks.","A Log Analytics workspace stores and queries data; it does not define alert responses.","A data collection rule defines what the Azure Monitor Agent collects, not what happens when an alert fires."],1],["adm84",0,5,"You want performance trends and a dependency map for a group of VMs. What should you enable?",["Network Watcher packet capture","Azure Advisor","VM insights with the Azure Monitor Agent","Blob storage analytics"],2,"VM insights uses the Azure Monitor Agent and a data collection rule to show performance and, optionally, process dependencies. Advisor gives recommendations, not trends.","Configure Azure Monitor insights",["Packet capture records network traffic on one VM; it gives neither performance trends nor a dependency map.","Advisor makes recommendations but does not chart VM performance over time or map dependencies.",null,"Storage analytics covers storage account requests and has nothing to do with VM performance."],2],["adm85",0,5,"You need to back up Azure VMs and Azure file shares. Which vault should you create?",["A Backup vault","A Key Vault","A storage account with GRS","A Recovery Services vault"],3,"Recovery Services vaults protect Azure VMs and Azure Files. Backup vaults cover workloads such as managed disks, blobs and PostgreSQL.","Create a Recovery Services vault",["A Backup vault protects workloads such as managed disks, blobs and Azure Database for PostgreSQL, not Azure VMs and Azure Files.","Key Vault stores secrets, keys and certificates and cannot hold backups.","A storage account with GRS is not a backup service and has no backup policies or recovery points.",null],1],["adm86",0,5,"A VM backup must be restorable in the paired region if the primary region is down. What must be set on the vault before backups are taken?",["LRS storage redundancy","GRS redundancy with Cross Region Restore enabled","A second vault in the same region","Soft delete disabled"],1,"Cross Region Restore requires a GRS vault with the feature enabled. LRS keeps data in one region only, and soft delete does not affect region restores.","Configure backup and restore",["LRS keeps backup data in one region, so it cannot be restored in the paired region.",null,"A second vault in the same region fails along with the primary region during a regional outage.","Disabling soft delete lowers protection against deleted backups and does nothing for cross-region restore."],3],["adm87",0,5,"A user deleted one spreadsheet from a VM protected by Azure Backup yesterday. What is the fastest way to recover just that file?",["Restore the entire VM to a new VM","Fail over the VM with Site Recovery to the paired region","Use File Recovery to mount the recovery point","Restore the managed disk and swap the OS disk"],2,"File Recovery mounts a recovery point as a drive through a script so you can copy single files. Restoring or failing over whole VMs takes far longer.","Perform restore operations",["Restoring the whole VM to recover one file is slow and creates a new VM you then need to clean up.","Site Recovery failover moves the whole workload to another region; it is for disasters, not single-file recovery.",null,"Restoring a disk and swapping the OS disk replaces the whole disk and takes far longer than mounting a recovery point."],2],["adm88",0,5,"A critical VM needs backups every 4 hours. What should you use?",["An Enhanced backup policy with hourly frequency","A Standard policy with daily backups","A lifecycle management rule","A snapshot taken manually each day"],0,"Enhanced policies support multiple backups per day (hourly schedules). Standard policies allow one backup a day.","Configure backup policies",[null,"A Standard policy allows only one backup a day, which cannot meet a 4-hour schedule.","Lifecycle management rules act on blobs in storage accounts and cannot back up a VM.","A daily manual snapshot runs only once a day and is not a managed backup schedule."],2],["adm89",0,5,"You must prove your disaster recovery plan for replicated VMs works without affecting production or ongoing replication. What should you run?",["A planned failover","An on-demand backup","A test failover into an isolated VNet","A restore to an alternate location"],2,"A test failover in Site Recovery creates VMs in an isolated network while replication continues. A planned failover moves production.","Configure Azure Site Recovery",["A planned failover moves production to the secondary region, which is exactly the impact the drill must avoid.","An on-demand backup protects data but does not test that replicated VMs can start in the recovery region.",null,"Restoring from backup to another location tests backups, not Site Recovery replication and failover."],2],["adm90",0,5,"After a Site Recovery failover to the secondary region, the primary region is healthy again. What should happen before failing back?",["Delete the recovery plan and create it again","Re-protect the VMs to reverse replication","Disable replication permanently","Convert the vault to a Backup vault"],1,"Re-protect starts replication in the reverse direction so you can fail back later. Deleting plans or disabling replication loses protection.","Perform failover to a secondary region",["Deleting and recreating the recovery plan does not restart replication, and you would lose the plan's configuration.",null,"Disabling replication leaves the VMs unprotected and makes a managed failback impossible.","Site Recovery runs from a Recovery Services vault; it cannot be converted to a Backup vault, and that would not reverse replication."],3],["x1",0,1,"A user holds the Global Administrator role in Microsoft Entra ID but gets an authorization error when trying to add a role assignment on a subscription. What is the supported way for this user to gain the ability to manage access to all subscriptions in the tenant?",["Assign themselves Owner at the tenant root management group from the Roles and administrators page in the Entra admin center","Add themselves to the subscription's Reader role, which picks up write rights from their Entra role","Turn on 'Access management for Azure resources' in the Entra ID properties to get User Access Administrator at root scope","Create a custom Microsoft Entra role that includes role assignment permissions"],2,"Global Administrators do not automatically hold Azure RBAC rights. Elevating access with 'Access management for Azure resources' grants User Access Administrator at the root scope (/), which covers all subscriptions. The Entra Roles and administrators page manages directory roles, not Azure RBAC assignments.","Domain 1: Entra roles vs Azure RBAC",["The Entra Roles and administrators page assigns directory roles; it cannot create Azure RBAC assignments at the root management group.","Reader grants only read access, and Azure RBAC does not inherit write rights from Entra directory roles.",null,"Entra custom roles cover directory permissions only; they cannot hold Azure RBAC actions such as role assignment writes."],3],["x2",0,1,"Your company wants employees to be able to invite B2B guest users, but only from the email domains of three approved partner companies. What should you configure?",["Collaboration restrictions in External collaboration settings that allow invitations only to the listed domains","A Conditional Access policy that blocks guest sign-ins from every location except the countries where the partners operate","A dynamic group rule that removes guests whose email address is not from an approved domain","An Azure Policy assignment with the Deny effect targeting guest user objects"],0,"External collaboration settings include collaboration restrictions that allow invitations only to specified domains (or block specified domains). Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions after an account exists; it does not restrict which domains can be invited.","Domain 1: External (B2B) users",[null,"Conditional Access applies at sign-in; it does not control which email domains employees can send invitations to.","Dynamic group rules only change group membership; they do not stop guests from being invited or remove them from the tenant.","Azure Policy governs Azure resources, not Microsoft Entra directory objects such as guest users."],2],["x3",0,1,"The built-in 'Allowed locations' policy is assigned at the subscription scope. A lab resource group, RG-Lab, must not be evaluated by this assignment at all. What should you do?",["Apply a CanNotDelete lock to RG-Lab","Assign a second copy of the policy to RG-Lab with the Audit effect","Recreate RG-Lab in one of the allowed regions","Add RG-Lab as an exclusion (not scope) on the policy assignment"],3,"Exclusions (notScopes) on an assignment remove a child scope from evaluation entirely. Assigning a second policy with Audit at RG-Lab adds another evaluation; it does not cancel the Deny inherited from the subscription assignment.","Domain 1: Azure Policy scopes and exclusions",["A lock prevents deletion of resources; it has no effect on whether a policy assignment evaluates the group.","A second assignment only adds more evaluation; the subscription assignment still applies to RG-Lab.","Recreating the group in an allowed region does not exclude it, and the resources inside it are still evaluated.",null],3],["x4",0,1,"A developer who has Contributor on RG1 cannot delete a storage account because of a CanNotDelete lock, and also cannot remove the lock. Which built-in role assigned on RG1 would let the developer remove the lock?",["Contributor, but assigned at the subscription scope instead of the resource group","Owner","Reader and Data Access","Storage Account Contributor"],1,"Removing a lock requires Microsoft.Authorization/locks/delete, which is included in Owner and User Access Administrator. Contributor at any scope excludes Microsoft.Authorization write and delete actions, so moving it to the subscription does not help.","Domain 1: Resource locks",["Contributor at any scope excludes Microsoft.Authorization write and delete actions, so it still cannot remove locks.",null,"Reader and Data Access lets users read storage account keys; it grants no rights over management locks.","Storage Account Contributor manages storage accounts but does not include permission to delete locks."],2],["x5",0,1,"New and existing resources should carry the CostCenter tag value of their resource group, and the tag must be on the resources themselves, not only in cost reports. What should you do?",["Enable tag inheritance in Cost Management settings for the subscription","Add the tag to the resource group, since resources inherit group tags","Assign the built-in 'Inherit a tag from the resource group' policy and run a remediation task","Apply a ReadOnly lock to the resource group so its tags cannot be removed"],2,"The Modify-effect policy 'Inherit a tag from the resource group' writes the tag on new or updated resources, and a remediation task fixes existing ones. Cost Management tag inheritance only applies group tags to cost records, not to the resources themselves.","Domain 1: Tags and policy",["Cost Management tag inheritance only stamps tags on cost records; the resources themselves stay untagged.","Resources never inherit tags from their resource group automatically.",null,"A ReadOnly lock blocks changes to the group; it does not copy any tags to the resources inside it."],3],["x6",0,1,"Operators must be able to start, restart and deallocate VMs in a subscription, but must not create, resize or delete any VM. What is the best approach?",["Create a custom RBAC role whose actions include the VM start, restart and deallocate actions plus read","Assign Virtual Machine Contributor, because it excludes delete operations","Assign Reader at the subscription and add a CanNotDelete lock to each virtual machine so deletes fail","Assign Virtual Machine User Login on the subscription"],0,"A custom role with only Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/start/action, restart/action, deallocate/action and read gives exactly the needed rights. Virtual Machine Contributor can create, resize and delete VMs, so it grants far more than required.","Domain 1: Custom RBAC roles",[null,"Virtual Machine Contributor can create, resize and delete VMs, which goes well beyond the stated requirement.","Reader cannot start or deallocate VMs, and locks only block deletes, not creating or resizing VMs.","Virtual Machine User Login allows signing in to a VM's OS with Entra ID; it cannot start or deallocate VMs."],3],["x7",0,1,"A service desk employee must reset passwords for non-administrator users in Microsoft Entra ID and should get no other administrative rights. Which role fits best?",["User Access Administrator on the subscription","Contributor at the tenant root management group","Global Administrator","Password Administrator"],3,"Password Administrator is a Microsoft Entra role that can reset passwords for non-administrators and a limited set of admins. User Access Administrator is an Azure RBAC role for managing resource access and has no rights over user passwords.","Domain 1: Entra roles",["User Access Administrator manages Azure resource role assignments and cannot reset Entra user passwords.","Contributor is an Azure RBAC role over resources and grants nothing in the Entra directory.","Global Administrator can reset passwords but grants full control of the tenant, violating least privilege.",null],2],["x8",0,1,"You need to move a storage account from subscription A to subscription B. Which condition must be met for the move to succeed?",["The storage account must be converted to LRS before it can leave the subscription","Both subscriptions are in the same tenant and the target has the provider registered","The destination resource group must be in the same region as the storage account","Any lock on the source group must be changed to ReadOnly"],1,"Moving resources between subscriptions requires both to be in the same Microsoft Entra tenant and the resource provider to be registered in the destination. The resource group's location is only metadata, so it does not have to match the resource's region.","Domain 1: Moving resources",["Redundancy has nothing to do with whether a storage account can move between subscriptions.",null,"A resource group's location is only where its metadata lives; resources in it can be in any region.","Locks on the source or target block moves; changing the lock to ReadOnly would still block it."],2],["x9",0,1,"A production VM of a fixed size in one region will run around the clock for the next three years and cannot be interrupted. Which option lowers its compute cost the most?",["Convert it to an Azure Spot VM, which can be evicted when Azure needs capacity","Scale it up during business hours and down at night","Buy a 3-year reservation for that VM size and region","Create a Cost Management budget with an alert at 100%"],2,"A reservation commits to a VM size and region for one or three years in exchange for a discount over pay-as-you-go, ideal for steady workloads. Spot VMs are cheaper but can be evicted, which the no-interruption requirement rules out.","Domain 1: Reservations and cost",["Spot VMs can be evicted at any time, which conflicts with a workload that must not be interrupted.","Resizing daily adds reboots and effort and does not approach the discount of a long-term commitment.",null,"A budget only raises alerts about spending; it does not reduce the price paid for compute."],2],["x10",0,1,"A policy that denies creation of public IP addresses is assigned to management group MG-Prod. An Owner of a subscription under MG-Prod tries to create a public IP. What happens?",["The request is denied, because the policy applies to every subscription under the management group","It succeeds, because the Owner role at the subscription scope overrides Deny effects assigned higher up","It succeeds unless a ReadOnly lock is also present on the subscription","It is denied only if the user's Owner role is also assigned at MG-Prod"],0,"Policy assignments are inherited by all child scopes, and Deny applies regardless of the caller's RBAC role, including Owner. RBAC decides who may attempt an action; policy decides whether the resultant configuration is allowed.","Domain 1: Management groups and inheritance",[null,"No RBAC role, including Owner, overrides a policy Deny effect; policy is evaluated independently of RBAC.","Locks are unrelated to policy evaluation; the Deny effect blocks the request by itself.","Policy applies based on the resource's scope, not on where the caller's role assignment was made."],2],["x11",0,1,"Finance wants to see last month's Azure spending for a subscription broken down by the value of the Department tag. Where should they look?",["Azure Advisor cost recommendations","The activity log filtered to show write operations that changed the Department tag","The Azure Policy compliance dashboard","Cost analysis in Cost Management, grouped by the tag"],3,"Cost analysis can group and filter actual and amortized costs by tag, resource group, service and more. The activity log records control-plane operations such as tag changes, but it contains no cost data.","Domain 1: Cost analysis",["Advisor suggests ways to save money; it does not report historical spending grouped by tag.","The activity log shows who changed what, not how much any resource cost.","Policy compliance shows which resources meet rules such as required tags, not the costs associated with them.",null],1],["x12",0,1,"Self-service password reset is enabled for all users. Security wants users to prove their identity with two different methods before a reset is allowed. What should you change?",["Create a Conditional Access policy that requires a compliant device for the reset portal","Set the number of methods required to reset to 2 in SSPR settings","Enable group-based licensing for the SSPR users","Turn on security defaults and turn off SSPR registration"],1,"SSPR's Authentication methods settings let you require one or two methods to reset a password. A Conditional Access compliant-device requirement does not configure how many verification methods the SSPR flow demands.","Domain 1: Self-service password reset",["A compliant-device requirement does not change how many verification methods SSPR asks for.",null,"Licensing assignment is unrelated to how many authentication methods a password reset requires.","Turning off SSPR registration would stop users from resetting their own passwords at all."],1],["x13",0,1,"An admin wants a single dynamic group that contains every user in the Sales department and every device those users own. What should the admin know?",["It works if the group is created as a Microsoft 365 group instead of a security group","It works if the membership rule uses the -any operator across users and devices","A dynamic rule selects either users or devices, so two separate groups are needed","Dynamic groups only support device rules, so the users must be added manually"],2,"A dynamic membership rule applies to either users or devices, never both in one group, so you create one dynamic user group and one dynamic device group. Microsoft 365 groups can only contain users, so switching group type does not help.","Domain 1: Dynamic groups",["Microsoft 365 groups can contain only users, so switching group type cannot add devices.","The -any operator works on multi-valued attributes within one object type; it cannot mix users and devices.",null,"Dynamic groups support both user rules and device rules, just not both in the same group."],3],["x14",0,1,"You assign a Deny policy that blocks storage accounts without secure transfer. Several existing storage accounts already have secure transfer turned off. What happens to them?",["They stay in place and are flagged non-compliant; only new create or update requests are denied","They are deleted during the next policy evaluation cycle","They are automatically reconfigured to require secure transfer during the next compliance evaluation scan","They become read-only until an owner fixes them"],0,"Deny acts on create and update requests; existing non-compliant resources are reported in compliance results but not changed or removed. Automatic fixing requires Modify or DeployIfNotExists with remediation, which Deny does not do.","Domain 1: Policy effects",[null,"Azure Policy never deletes resources because they are non-compliant.","Only Modify or DeployIfNotExists policies with remediation change resources; Deny does not alter existing ones.","Policy does not place resources in a read-only state; that would require a ReadOnly lock."],2],["x15",0,2,"Compliance archives are read about once a quarter, must be readable immediately without waiting for rehydration, and are kept for years. Which blob access tier gives the lowest storage cost that meets this?",["Archive","Hot","Cool","Cold"],3,"Cold is an online tier with lower storage cost than Cool, suited to data kept at least 90 days and accessed rarely, and it can be read immediately. Archive is cheaper still but is offline and needs rehydration, which breaks the immediate-read requirement.","Domain 2: Access tiers",["Archive is offline, so reading a blob requires rehydration that can take hours, breaking the immediate-read need.","Hot has the highest storage cost and is meant for frequently accessed data, not quarterly reads.","Cool works but costs more to store than Cold for data kept long term and read this rarely.",null],3],["x16",0,2,"Hybrid users on Microsoft Entra joined Windows devices must access an Azure file share with identity-based authentication. The devices have no network line of sight to domain controllers. Which identity source should you enable on the storage account?",["On-premises AD DS authentication for Azure Files","Microsoft Entra Kerberos for hybrid identities","The storage account key mapped with net use","Microsoft Entra Domain Services"],1,"Microsoft Entra Kerberos issues Kerberos tickets for Azure Files from Entra ID, so clients do not need to reach a domain controller. AD DS authentication requires clients to contact on-premises domain controllers to obtain tickets.","Domain 2: Identity-based access for Azure Files",["AD DS authentication needs clients to reach a domain controller for Kerberos tickets, which these devices cannot do.",null,"Mounting with the account key bypasses identity-based authentication and gives full access to the share.","Entra Domain Services is a separate managed domain; the devices would still need line of sight to its domain controllers."],3],["x17",0,2,"Identity-based access is enabled for an Azure file share. Every authenticated user should get read/write share-level access without individual role assignments, with NTFS ACLs doing the fine-grained control. What should you configure?",["Assign Storage Blob Data Contributor to all users on the account","Assign Contributor on the storage account to the Domain Users group","Set the default share-level permission to Storage File Data SMB Share Contributor","Skip share-level permissions and rely only on NTFS ACLs"],2,"A default share-level permission applies a share-level role to all authenticated identities, after which NTFS ACLs restrict access. Contributor is a control-plane role and grants no SMB data access; it would also let users change the account.","Domain 2: Share-level permissions",["Storage Blob Data Contributor grants blob data access, not SMB access to Azure file shares.","Contributor is a control-plane role that manages the account but grants no SMB share data access.",null,"Share-level permission is required; without it, users are denied before NTFS ACLs are even evaluated."],2],["x18",0,2,"After you regenerate key1 on a storage account, several users report that their SAS URLs stopped working. Which SAS tokens were invalidated?",["Account and service SAS tokens that were signed with key1","Only user delegation SAS tokens","Every SAS token issued for the account, including the ones signed with key2","None; SAS tokens do not depend on the account keys"],0,"Account and service SAS tokens are signed with an account key, so regenerating that key invalidates tokens signed with it. User delegation SAS tokens are secured with Entra credentials and a user delegation key, not the account key.","Domain 2: Shared access signatures",[null,"User delegation SAS tokens are signed with a user delegation key from Entra ID, so key rotation leaves them working.","Tokens signed with key2 keep working because only key1 was regenerated.","Account and service SAS signatures are computed with an account key, so regenerating that key breaks them."],2],["x19",0,2,"You created a private endpoint for a storage account's blob service, but VMs in the VNet still resolve the account name to its public IP. What is most likely missing?",["A service endpoint for Microsoft.Storage on the VM subnet","A storage firewall IP rule that allows the public IP addresses of the VMs","An outbound NSG rule for the Storage service tag","A linked privatelink.blob.core.windows.net private DNS zone"],3,"Name resolution to the private IP comes from the privatelink private DNS zone (or equivalent DNS) linked to the VNet with an A record for the account. A service endpoint is a different feature that keeps the public IP and does not change DNS.","Domain 2: Private endpoints for storage",["A service endpoint keeps the public IP and does not make the name resolve to the private endpoint's address.","A firewall rule for public IPs does not change DNS, so traffic would still go to the public endpoint.","Allowing outbound traffic to the service tag does nothing about the name resolving to the public IP.",null],3],["x20",0,2,"An admin deleted an Azure file share by mistake yesterday. Which setting, if it was enabled beforehand, lets you restore the share?",["Blob versioning","Soft delete for file shares","Container soft delete on the storage account","The blob change feed"],1,"Soft delete for Azure file shares keeps deleted shares for the retention period so they can be undeleted. Container soft delete protects blob containers, not file shares.","Domain 2: Azure Files data protection",["Blob versioning protects blob contents in containers; it does not apply to Azure file shares.",null,"Container soft delete restores deleted blob containers, not file shares.","The change feed logs blob changes; it cannot restore a deleted file share."],1],["x21",0,2,"An audit team needs an ordered, durable log of every create, modify and delete event on blobs in an account, which a process can read later. What should you enable?",["Blob inventory reports","Blob index tags","Change feed","Lifecycle management"],2,"The change feed records blob change events in order as a durable, read-only log. Blob inventory produces periodic lists of blobs and properties, not a record of each change event.","Domain 2: Change feed",["Blob inventory gives scheduled lists of blobs and their properties, not an ordered record of each change.","Blob index tags are key-value attributes for finding blobs; they do not log events.",null,"Lifecycle management moves or deletes blobs by rules; it does not record changes."],2],["x22",0,2,"A project manager with no scripting experience needs to browse containers, upload a few files and edit blob properties from a desktop application. What should you recommend?",["Azure Storage Explorer","AzCopy with the sync command","Azure Data Box","Azure Import/Export"],0,"Storage Explorer is a free graphical desktop tool for browsing and managing blobs, files, queues and tables. AzCopy is a command-line tool, which does not fit someone who does not script.","Domain 2: Storage Explorer and AzCopy",[null,"AzCopy is a command-line tool, which does not suit someone without scripting experience.","Data Box is a physical appliance for bulk offline transfer, far too heavy for a few files.","Import/Export ships your own disks to a datacenter, which is not for browsing or editing blobs."],1],["x23",0,2,"Before running a risky script, an admin wants a manual, read-only, point-in-time copy of one specific blob that can be restored later. What should they create?",["An entry in the change feed","An Archive tier copy of the container","A container soft delete policy","A blob snapshot"],3,"A snapshot is a read-only version of a blob taken at a point in time on demand, and it can be promoted back over the base blob. Container soft delete only helps if the whole container is deleted.","Domain 2: Blob snapshots",["The change feed records events about changes but holds no copy of the blob's content.","Archive is an access tier, not a backup; copying the whole container to Archive is slow to restore and excessive.","Container soft delete only helps if the whole container is deleted, not if a blob is changed.",null],1],["x24",0,2,"A script needs one SAS that allows listing containers in the blob service and creating queues in the queue service of the same storage account. Which SAS type supports this?",["A user delegation SAS scoped to one container","An account SAS","A service SAS for the blob service","A stored access policy on a queue"],1,"An account SAS can span multiple services and grant service-level operations such as listing containers or creating queues. A service SAS covers resources in only one service, so it cannot grant queue creation alongside blob listing.","Domain 2: SAS types",["A user delegation SAS is limited to blob resources and cannot create queues.",null,"A service SAS covers only one service, so it cannot also grant queue creation.","A stored access policy is a set of constraints for service SAS tokens on one resource, not a cross-service SAS."],2],["x25",0,2,"A container's anonymous access level is set to Blob, yet anonymous requests for its blobs are refused. What explains this?",["Anonymous access only works when the container is in the Cool tier","Anonymous access is not supported on accounts that use ZRS","Anonymous blob access is disabled at the storage account level, which overrides the container setting","Anonymous access is only available for Azure Files shares"],2,"The account-level setting that allows blob anonymous access must be enabled; when it is disabled, no container can be read anonymously regardless of its own access level. Access tier and redundancy have no effect on anonymous access.","Domain 2: Configure access to storage",["Access tier has no effect on whether anonymous read access is permitted.","Redundancy settings such as ZRS do not affect anonymous access.",null,"Anonymous access is a blob feature; Azure Files shares do not support anonymous access."],3],["x26",0,2,"An application needs SMB file shares on SSD storage with low latency and provisioned performance. Which storage account should you create?",["A FileStorage account with Premium performance","A Standard general-purpose v2 account using the Hot tier","A BlockBlobStorage account with Premium performance","A Standard general-purpose v2 account with ZRS"],0,"Premium Azure file shares live in a FileStorage (Premium) account and run on SSDs. A BlockBlobStorage account is premium too, but it is for block blobs and does not host file shares.","Domain 2: Storage account types",[null,"A Standard account uses HDD-backed file shares without provisioned performance, and Hot is a blob tier.","BlockBlobStorage accounts are premium but hold block blobs only; they cannot host file shares.","ZRS affects redundancy, not performance; the shares would still be on Standard storage."],2],["x27",0,3,"You deploy an ARM template to a resource group without specifying a mode. The group contains a VM that is not in the template. What happens to the VM?",["It is deleted, because resources not in the template are removed","The deployment fails validation because the template does not list every existing resource","It is moved to a new resource group","It stays unchanged, because the default mode is incremental"],3,"Incremental is the default deployment mode: resources in the group but not in the template are left as they are. Only complete mode deletes resources that are missing from the template.","Domain 3: Deployment modes",["Only complete mode deletes resources that are not in the template; it is not the default.","Templates do not need to list existing resources; validation does not fail for that reason.","Deployments never move resources to other resource groups.",null],2],["x28",0,3,"In a Bicep file, a VM resource sets its network interface with nic.id, where nic is the symbolic name of a NIC declared in the same file. Do you need to add dependsOn?",["Yes, every resource must declare dependsOn explicitly","No, referencing the NIC's symbolic name creates an implicit dependency","Yes, otherwise the NIC is deployed to a different resource group","No, because Bicep always deploys resources one at a time in file order"],1,"Bicep infers dependencies when one resource references another's symbolic name or properties, so the NIC is deployed first. Bicep does not deploy in file order; resources without dependencies deploy in parallel.","Domain 3: Bicep files",["dependsOn is only needed when no reference already exists; Bicep infers most dependencies.",null,"Dependencies control ordering only; they do not decide the target resource group.","Bicep deploys independent resources in parallel, not one by one in file order."],2],["x29",0,3,"An app and a log-shipping sidecar must run on the same host, share a lifecycle and a volume, and talk over localhost, without running an orchestrator. What should you deploy?",["Two separate Azure Container Apps environments","Two web apps in one App Service plan","An Azure Container Instances container group","Two repositories in Azure Container Registry"],2,"A container group in ACI schedules containers on the same host with a shared lifecycle, local network and volumes, much like a pod. Two Container Apps environments are separate network boundaries, so the containers could not share localhost.","Domain 3: Azure Container Instances",["Separate Container Apps environments are separate network boundaries, so the containers cannot share localhost.","Two web apps run as separate apps and do not share a lifecycle or localhost networking.",null,"ACR stores images; it does not run containers at all."],2],["x30",0,3,"A team wants container images rebuilt automatically in Azure whenever code is committed or the base image is updated, without maintaining build agents. What should they use?",["ACR Tasks","Container Apps revisions","An ACI restart policy of Always","ACR geo-replication"],0,"ACR Tasks build images in Azure and can trigger on source commits and base image updates. Geo-replication copies a registry across regions; it does not build anything.","Domain 3: Azure Container Registry",[null,"Revisions are versions of a running Container Apps app; they do not build images.","A restart policy controls whether a container restarts after it exits; it does not build images.","Geo-replication copies a registry to other regions and has no build capability."],1],["x31",0,3,"You are releasing a new version of an Azure Container Apps app and want 20% of traffic sent to it while the old version keeps 80%. What should you do?",["Keep single revision mode and increase the replica count","Create a second Container Apps environment and use DNS round robin","Add an HTTP scale rule with a concurrency value of 20","Use multiple revision mode and set traffic weights of 80 and 20"],3,"Multiple revision mode keeps several revisions active and lets ingress split traffic by weight. In single revision mode the new revision replaces the old one, so no split is possible.","Domain 3: Azure Container Apps",["Single revision mode replaces the old revision with the new one, so traffic cannot be split.","DNS round robin cannot set a 20% share, and a second environment adds needless complexity.","Scale rules control replica counts, not how traffic is divided between versions.",null],2],["x32",0,3,"An existing standalone VM must be placed into an existing availability set with other web servers. What must you do?",["Stop the VM and pick the availability set in its Configuration settings","Recreate the VM in the availability set, for example from its disks","Move the VM into the availability set's resource group","Resize the VM to a size that supports availability sets"],1,"A VM can only be added to an availability set when it is created, so you recreate it (keeping its disks) inside the set. Moving resource groups changes only management grouping, not physical placement.","Domain 3: Availability sets",["The availability set can only be chosen at creation time; there is no setting to change it afterward.",null,"Moving to another resource group changes management grouping, not physical placement in a set.","No VM size change adds an existing VM to an availability set."],2],["x33",0,3,"A new Virtual Machine Scale Set must let admins manage instances with the standard VM APIs and mix Spot and regular-priority instances. Which orchestration mode should you choose?",["Uniform orchestration","An availability set with autoscale","Flexible orchestration","A proximity placement group"],2,"Flexible orchestration creates standard VM resources that use the normal VM APIs and supports mixing Spot and regular priority. Uniform mode manages identical instances through scale set APIs and is the tempting but wrong choice here.","Domain 3: Scale set orchestration modes",["Uniform mode uses scale set instance APIs rather than standard VM APIs, which does not meet the requirement.","Availability sets do not autoscale and are not a scale set orchestration mode.",null,"A proximity placement group only keeps resources close together for latency; it is not an orchestration mode."],3],["x34",0,3,"Compliance requires VM OS and data volumes to be encrypted inside the guest with BitLocker, with the keys kept in Azure Key Vault. What should you use?",["Azure Disk Encryption with a Key Vault","Encryption at host","Default server-side encryption with platform-managed keys","Infrastructure encryption on a storage account"],0,"Azure Disk Encryption uses BitLocker (Windows) or DM-Crypt (Linux) inside the guest and stores its secrets in Key Vault. Encryption at host encrypts on the Azure host without guest BitLocker, so it does not meet a requirement for in-guest encryption.","Domain 3: VM disk encryption",[null,"Encryption at host encrypts data on the Azure host, not with BitLocker inside the guest OS.","Server-side encryption happens at the storage layer, not inside the guest with BitLocker.","Infrastructure encryption applies to storage accounts, not managed disks inside a VM guest."],2],["x35",0,3,"A lightly used dev/test web server needs more consistent disk performance than HDD while keeping cost low. Which managed disk type fits best?",["Ultra Disk","Premium SSD v2","Standard HDD","Standard SSD"],3,"Standard SSD gives more consistent latency than Standard HDD at a low price, suited to light web and dev/test workloads. Ultra Disk and Premium SSD v2 target demanding, latency-sensitive workloads and cost more than needed.","Domain 3: Disk types",["Ultra Disk is aimed at the most demanding, latency-sensitive workloads and costs far more than needed.","Premium SSD v2 targets production workloads needing tunable performance, more than a light dev server needs.","Standard HDD is cheapest but has the less consistent performance the requirement wants to avoid.",null],2],["x36",0,3,"A web app on a Standard App Service plan gets heavy traffic every weekday morning. You want instances added when CPU is high and removed afterward. What should you configure?",["Scale up the plan to a Premium tier so each instance has more CPU and memory","Autoscale (scale out) rules on the App Service plan","An extra deployment slot for the busy hours","The Always On setting"],1,"Scaling out with autoscale rules changes the instance count based on metrics such as CPU. Scaling up only changes instance size and does not add or remove instances with demand.","Domain 3: App Service scaling",["Scaling up changes the instance size, not the number of instances, and does not react to demand.",null,"Deployment slots are for staging releases; they do not add serving capacity to production.","Always On keeps an app loaded when idle; it does not add instances."],1],["x37",0,3,"An App Service app uses the custom domain www.contoso.com. You need a TLS certificate for it at no certificate cost that renews automatically. What should you use?",["A self-signed certificate uploaded to the app","The default *.azurewebsites.net certificate bound to the custom domain","A free App Service managed certificate","An App Service Environment"],2,"App Service managed certificates are free and renewed automatically for custom domains on supported tiers. The *.azurewebsites.net certificate does not cover contoso.com, so browsers would show a name mismatch.","Domain 3: App Service TLS",["Self-signed certificates are not trusted by browsers, so users would see warnings.","The azurewebsites.net certificate does not include contoso.com, so browsers report a name mismatch.",null,"An App Service Environment is an isolated hosting option, not a certificate, and it is costly."],2],["x38",0,3,"A web app must accept inbound traffic only from an Application Gateway subnet in your VNet. What should you configure on the web app?",["An access restriction rule that allows only the gateway's subnet","Regional VNet integration with the virtual network that hosts the gateway","A network security group on the web app","A Hybrid Connection to the gateway"],0,"Access restrictions filter inbound traffic to the app, and a rule can allow a specific subnet by using a service endpoint. VNet integration affects outbound traffic from the app into a VNet, not who can reach it.","Domain 3: App Service networking",[null,"VNet integration lets the app make outbound calls into a VNet; it does not restrict inbound traffic.","NSGs attach to subnets and NICs, not directly to a multitenant App Service app.","Hybrid Connections let the app reach specific remote endpoints outbound; they do not filter inbound traffic."],3],["x39",0,3,"A web app must be reachable only from the internal network on a private IP, and public access must be turned off. What should you add?",["VNet integration for the app","Access restrictions that allow on-premises public IPs","A Hybrid Connection","A private endpoint for the web app"],3,"A private endpoint gives the app an inbound private IP in your VNet, and public network access can then be disabled. VNet integration only handles outbound calls from the app.","Domain 3: App Service private endpoints",["VNet integration handles outbound calls from the app, not inbound access over a private IP.","Access restrictions filter inbound traffic but keep the app on its public endpoint.","Hybrid Connections are for the app reaching endpoints elsewhere, not for private inbound access.",null],2],["x40",0,3,"Which Azure PowerShell cmdlet deploys a Bicep file to an existing resource group?",["New-AzDeployment","New-AzResourceGroupDeployment","New-AzTemplateSpec","Set-AzResourceGroup"],1,"New-AzResourceGroupDeployment deploys an ARM or Bicep template at resource group scope. New-AzDeployment targets subscription scope, so it is the tempting but wrong choice.","Domain 3: Deploy templates",["New-AzDeployment deploys at subscription scope, not into a resource group.",null,"New-AzTemplateSpec stores a template as a template spec resource; it does not deploy it.","Set-AzResourceGroup changes a group's tags; it does not deploy templates."],1],["x41",0,4,"How many IP addresses in a /29 subnet can you assign to VMs in an Azure virtual network?",["6","8","3","5"],2,"A /29 has 8 addresses and Azure reserves 5 in every subnet (network, gateway, two DNS, broadcast), leaving 3. The answer 6 applies classic networking rules that reserve only two addresses.","Domain 4: Subnets and reserved IPs",["6 assumes only the network and broadcast addresses are reserved, but Azure reserves 5 per subnet.","8 is the total size of a /29, ignoring the addresses Azure reserves in every subnet.",null,"5 is the number of addresses Azure reserves, not the number left for VMs."],1],["x42",0,4,"VNets in East US and West Europe need private, low-latency connectivity over the Microsoft backbone without deploying any gateways. What should you configure?",["Global VNet peering","A VNet-to-VNet VPN between two VPN gateways","Service endpoints on both VNets","Azure Bastion in each VNet"],0,"Global VNet peering connects VNets in different regions over the Microsoft backbone without gateways. A VNet-to-VNet VPN also connects them but needs a VPN gateway in each VNet.","Domain 4: Virtual network peering",[null,"A VNet-to-VNet VPN works but needs a VPN gateway in each VNet, which the requirement rules out.","Service endpoints give private routes to Azure services, not connectivity between two VNets.","Bastion provides remote RDP and SSH access to VMs; it does not connect VNets."],2],["x43",0,4,"Using routing rather than security rules, you must make traffic from a subnet to 10.50.0.0/16 be dropped. What should you create?",["An NSG outbound deny rule for 10.50.0.0/16","A route with next hop Virtual network gateway","A route with next hop Internet","A route for 10.50.0.0/16 with next hop None"],3,"A user-defined route with next hop type None drops matching traffic. An NSG rule also blocks it, but it is a security rule, which the requirement excludes.","Domain 4: User-defined routes",["An NSG rule would block the traffic but is a security rule, which the requirement excludes.","Next hop Virtual network gateway would forward traffic toward the gateway instead of dropping it.","Next hop Internet would try to send the traffic out to the internet rather than drop it.",null],3],["x44",0,4,"All internet-bound traffic from Azure subnets must be sent to the on-premises network over the site-to-site VPN for inspection. What should you configure?",["A route table with 0.0.0.0/0 and next hop Internet","Forced tunneling with a 0.0.0.0/0 route to the virtual network gateway","Service endpoints for Microsoft.Storage and Microsoft.Sql on every subnet in the VNet","A NAT gateway on each subnet"],1,"Forced tunneling sends the default route to the VPN gateway, so internet traffic goes to on-premises. A NAT gateway sends traffic straight to the internet from Azure, which skips the on-premises inspection.","Domain 4: Forced tunneling",["Next hop Internet keeps traffic going straight to the internet from Azure, skipping on-premises inspection.",null,"Service endpoints route traffic to Azure services privately; they do not send internet traffic on-premises.","A NAT gateway provides outbound internet from Azure directly, bypassing on-premises."],2],["x45",0,4,"A VM has one NSG on its subnet and another on its NIC. You want to see the combined list of rules that actually applies to the VM. What should you open?",["IP flow verify","Connection Monitor","Effective security rules for the NIC","Virtual network flow logs"],2,"Effective security rules show the aggregated rules from the subnet and NIC NSGs as applied to the NIC. IP flow verify tests one specific flow and reports the deciding rule, not the full combined rule list.","Domain 4: Effective security rules",["IP flow verify tests one specific flow and names the deciding rule, not the full combined rule list.","Connection Monitor tracks reachability and latency over time; it does not list NSG rules.",null,"Flow logs record traffic that happened; they do not show the rule set in effect."],2],["x46",0,4,"Admins want to connect to VMs through Azure Bastion by using the native RDP client on their workstations. The current Bastion is Basic SKU. What should you do?",["Upgrade Bastion to Standard SKU and enable native client support","Redeploy Bastion as the Developer SKU, which is designed for individual admin connections","Assign a public IP to each VM and allow RDP","Enable IP-based connection on the Basic SKU"],0,"Native client support is available on the Standard SKU and above and must be enabled in the Bastion configuration. The Developer SKU is a lower tier that does not offer native client connections.","Domain 4: Azure Bastion",[null,"The Developer SKU is a lower tier that does not support native client connections.","Public IPs with open RDP expose the VMs to the internet, the opposite of Bastion's purpose.","IP-based connection is not available on Basic, and it does not add native client support anyway."],3],["x47",0,4,"The apex of contoso.com in Azure DNS must point to the public IP of an Azure load balancer, and the record should update automatically if that IP resource changes. What should you create?",["A CNAME record at the zone apex","An A record set that contains the load balancer's current public IP address","An NS record pointing to the load balancer","An alias record set that targets the public IP resource"],3,"Alias record sets reference an Azure resource directly, follow changes to its IP and are allowed at the zone apex. A CNAME is not allowed at the apex because it cannot coexist with the zone's SOA and NS records.","Domain 4: Azure DNS alias records",["CNAME records are not allowed at the zone apex because they cannot coexist with SOA and NS records.","A static A record would not update automatically if the public IP resource changes.","NS records delegate a zone to name servers; they cannot point to a load balancer.",null],3],["x48",0,4,"Admins must SSH to individual backend VMs through a public Standard Load Balancer, using port 50001 for VM1 and 50002 for VM2. What should you configure?",["A load-balancing rule with session persistence","Inbound NAT rules","An outbound rule","A health probe on port 22"],1,"Inbound NAT rules map a frontend port to a specific backend VM and port, such as 50001 to VM1:22. A load-balancing rule spreads traffic across the pool and cannot target a single VM.","Domain 4: Load balancer NAT rules",["Load-balancing rules spread traffic across the pool and cannot send a port to a single chosen VM.",null,"Outbound rules configure SNAT for outbound traffic, not inbound access to specific VMs.","Health probes check backend health; they do not create inbound access paths."],2],["x49",0,4,"A legacy app behind an Azure Load Balancer keeps session state in memory, so each client must keep reaching the same backend VM. What should you change?",["Shorten the health probe interval","Enable floating IP on the rule","Set session persistence to client IP on the load-balancing rule","Add an inbound NAT rule for each client so its traffic always maps to one VM"],2,"Session persistence (client IP or client IP and protocol) sends a client's requests to the same backend. Floating IP changes how the frontend IP is presented to the backend, not client affinity.","Domain 4: Load balancing rules",["Probe interval affects how fast failures are detected, not which backend a client reaches.","Floating IP changes how the frontend IP is presented to backends, not client affinity.",null,"Inbound NAT rules map ports to specific VMs for admin access; they cannot be created per client."],2],["x50",0,4,"From a VM, you need to test a TCP connection to an external FQDN on port 443 and see where along the path it fails, including hop latency. Which Network Watcher tool should you use?",["Connection troubleshoot","IP flow verify","Next hop","Effective routes"],0,"Connection troubleshoot tests connectivity from a VM to a destination address or FQDN and port, and reports hops, latency and the reason for failure. IP flow verify only checks whether NSG rules allow a single packet at the VM.","Domain 4: Network Watcher",[null,"IP flow verify only checks NSG rules for one packet at the VM and cannot test a remote FQDN.","Next hop shows the routing decision for one destination, not whether a TCP connection succeeds.","Effective routes list the routes on a NIC but do not test the connection or report hop latency."],3],["x51",0,4,"An NSG outbound rule must allow VMs to reach Azure Storage in East US only, without listing IP ranges that Microsoft changes over time. What should the destination be?",["An application security group named Storage","A user-defined route to the storage endpoint","A private DNS zone for blob.core.windows.net","The Storage.EastUS service tag"],3,"Service tags such as Storage.EastUS represent Microsoft-maintained IP prefixes and can be used in NSG rules. Application security groups group your own VM NICs; they cannot represent an Azure service.","Domain 4: NSGs and service tags",["Application security groups group your own VM NICs; they cannot represent Azure service addresses.","Routes control where traffic goes, not whether an NSG allows it.","A private DNS zone affects name resolution and cannot be a destination in an NSG rule.",null],2],["x52",0,5,"An engineer wants to chart a VM's CPU percentage over the past two weeks right now, without creating a Log Analytics workspace. Where should they look?",["A KQL query in a Log Analytics workspace","Metrics explorer on the VM's platform metrics","The subscription activity log","A diagnostic setting that sends data to storage"],1,"Platform metrics such as Percentage CPU are collected automatically and can be charted in Metrics explorer with no setup. A Log Analytics query needs a workspace and data collection configured first.","Domain 5: Metrics vs logs",["A KQL query needs a workspace and collected data, which the engineer wants to avoid setting up.",null,"The activity log records control-plane operations, not performance counters such as CPU.","Diagnostic settings export logs and metrics elsewhere; they are not a charting tool."],2],["x53",0,5,"An alert should fire when a specific error string appears in application logs in a Log Analytics workspace more than five times in 15 minutes. Which alert type should you create?",["A metric alert","An activity log alert","A log search alert rule with a KQL query","A Service Health alert"],2,"Log search alerts run a KQL query on a schedule and fire when the results meet a threshold. Metric alerts evaluate numeric metrics and cannot search log text.","Domain 5: Alert rules",["Metric alerts evaluate numeric metrics and cannot search log text for a string.","Activity log alerts react to control-plane events, not application log content.",null,"Service Health alerts report Azure service incidents, not your application's errors."],2],["x54",0,5,"The operations team wants to be notified when Azure reports a service issue or planned maintenance that affects services and regions they use. What should they create?",["An activity log alert on Service Health events","A metric alert on VM availability","An Azure Advisor weekly digest","A log search alert on the Heartbeat table"],0,"Service Health alerts are activity log alerts on Service Health events, filtered by service, region and event type. Advisor digests summarize recommendations and do not report service incidents.","Domain 5: Alerts and Service Health",[null,"A VM availability metric reflects one VM, not Azure-wide incidents or planned maintenance.","Advisor digests summarize best-practice recommendations, not service incidents.","Heartbeat data shows agent connectivity for machines, not Azure service health events."],2],["x55",0,5,"Windows Security events must be collected from 50 VMs with the Azure Monitor Agent into a Log Analytics workspace. What should you configure?",["The legacy Log Analytics agent on each VM","A diagnostic setting on each VM resource","Boot diagnostics on each VM","A data collection rule associated with the VMs"],3,"With the Azure Monitor Agent, data collection rules define which events and counters to collect and where to send them, and are associated with the VMs. Diagnostic settings send platform resource logs, not guest operating system events.","Domain 5: Azure Monitor Agent and DCRs",["The legacy Log Analytics agent is retired; the Azure Monitor Agent is configured through DCRs instead.","Diagnostic settings collect platform logs for a resource; they do not collect Windows events from the guest.","Boot diagnostics captures console output and screenshots, not Windows Security events.",null],2],["x56",0,5,"You need to protect Azure managed disks with Azure Disk Backup and also back up blobs in a storage account. Which vault should you create?",["A Recovery Services vault","A Backup vault","A Key Vault","A Site Recovery vault in the paired region"],1,"Azure Disk Backup and Azure Blob backup are managed from a Backup vault. A Recovery Services vault protects workloads such as Azure VMs, file shares and SQL in VMs, and is the tempting but wrong choice here.","Domain 5: Backup vault vs Recovery Services vault",["Recovery Services vaults protect VMs, file shares and databases, but disk and blob backup use a Backup vault.",null,"Key Vault stores keys and secrets; it does not hold backups.","Site Recovery replicates VMs for disaster recovery; it is not how disk or blob backup is configured."],3],["x57",0,5,"An attacker with access to the portal stopped backup for a VM and deleted its backup data. Soft delete was left at its default settings. What can you do?",["Nothing; deleting backup data is immediate and permanent","Recover it only if Cross Region Restore was enabled","Undelete the backup item during the soft delete retention period and resume protection","Restore the data from the activity log"],2,"Soft delete for VM backups keeps deleted backup data for a retention period (14 days by default) so it can be undeleted. Cross Region Restore copies data to the paired region but is not required to recover soft-deleted items.","Domain 5: Backup soft delete",["With soft delete on (the default), deleted backup data is kept for a retention period, not removed immediately.","Cross Region Restore copies data to the paired region; it is not needed to undelete soft-deleted items.",null,"The activity log records operations; it does not contain backup data to restore."],2],["x58",0,5,"After failing a VM over to the secondary region with Azure Site Recovery and confirming it works, which action finalizes the failover and removes the other recovery points?",["Commit","Re-protect","Clean up test failover","Planned failover"],0,"Commit completes the failover and discards the remaining recovery points, so you can no longer pick a different one. Re-protect starts replication back toward the original region and comes afterward.","Domain 5: Azure Site Recovery",[null,"Re-protect starts replication back toward the original region; it does not finalize the failover.","Cleaning up a test failover removes test VMs; it does not apply to a real failover.","Planned failover is a type of failover from a healthy primary, not the step that finalizes one."],1],["x59",0,5,"You want backup reports that cover several Recovery Services vaults. What must you configure first?",["Cross Region Restore on each vault","An action group for each vault","Soft delete on each vault","Diagnostic settings that send vault data to a Log Analytics workspace"],3,"Backup reports are built from data the vaults send to a Log Analytics workspace through diagnostic settings. Action groups deliver alert notifications and do not feed reports.","Domain 5: Backup reports",["Cross Region Restore enables restores in the paired region; it does not produce report data.","Action groups send alert notifications; they do not feed reports.","Soft delete protects deleted backup data; it is unrelated to reporting.",null],3],["x60",0,5,"You need to continuously track reachability and latency from several Azure VMs to an on-premises endpoint over time, with alerts when checks fail. What should you use?",["IP flow verify","Connection Monitor","Next hop","Packet capture"],1,"Connection Monitor runs ongoing tests between sources and destinations, records latency and failures, and can raise alerts. IP flow verify is a one-time check of NSG rules for a single flow.","Domain 5: Connection Monitor",["IP flow verify is a one-time check of NSG rules for a single flow, not ongoing monitoring.",null,"Next hop shows one routing decision at a moment in time, not ongoing reachability or latency.","Packet capture records traffic for a limited session; it does not continuously test reachability or alert."],2]]);
